var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Concepts","page":"Concepts","text":"","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Why-a-quiver","page":"Concepts","text":"A recipe is a list: ingredients, a mash schedule, a boil schedule, a fermentation. It describes one wort that becomes one beer. Many brews do not fit a list: a parti-gyle takes several worts of decreasing strength from the same mash; a double mash runs the wort of one mash through another; a brewery blends the beers of several fermentors into products of its own. In all of them, streams split and merge.\n\nBrewQuiver describes a brew as a quiver, the mathematicians' name for a directed multigraph: a set of nodes, and arrows between them, with possibly several arrows joining the same two nodes. Here the graph is acyclic, since brewing goes forward in time.\n\nA node is the contents of a vessel at a stage of the brew: the pale malt on the shelf, the strike water, the mash before and after its rest, the wort in the kettle before and after the boil, the beer in the fermentor.\nAn arrow is a process taking contents from a node to another: a transfer, a drain, or a transformation (a mash rest, a boil, a fermentation, ...).","title":"Why a quiver"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Contents:-conserved-quantities","page":"Concepts","text":"The state of a node is a Contents: a Liquor and a list of solids. Both hold conserved quantities only:\n\nLiquor field What it is Unit\nwater mass of water kg\nextract mass of dissolved extract (sugars, dextrins) kg\nalcohol mass of ethanol kg\nisoalpha mass of iso-alpha acids mg\ncolor color units lb·°L\nco2 mass of dissolved CO₂ g\ntemperature temperature °C\nminerals dissolved ions (Minerals) mg\n\nSolids are ingredients as added (Portion, with an amount and a timing), spent grain (Residue), hop matter (HopMatter) and yeast (YeastCake).\n\nBecause every field is an amount, mixing is addition (a + b, with the temperature of the mixture) and splitting is scaling (0.3 * c), and both are exact. Everything a brewer usually reads off a wort, its volume at 20 °C, gravity, original gravity, ABV, IBU, color, carbonation, is derived from these amounts with BrewingFormulas:\n\nusing Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BrewQuiver\n\nw = wort(20u\"L\", 1.050u\"sg\")\n(volume(w), gravity(w), w.extract)\n(20.0 L, 1.05 sg, 2.5967265319537507 kg)\n\nhalf = 0.5 * w\n(volume(half), gravity(half))\n(10.0 L, 1.05 sg)\n\ndiluted = w + water(5u\"L\")\ngravity(diluted)\n1.04005443074054 sg","title":"Contents: conserved quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Nodes","page":"Concepts","text":"Nodes are labelled by symbols and added with add!:\n\nlots have initial contents: an ingredient and an amount (pale => 4.5u\"kg\", us05 => 1 for a count), or a liquor such as water(16u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\");\nstages start empty and are filled by arrows: add!(q, :mash, :rested; vessel = :mashtun). The vessel tags the stages that live in the same vessel, for its equipment and the timeline.\n\nWhatever arrives at a node is mixed, so blending needs no special process.","title":"Nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Arrows","page":"Concepts","text":"Arrows are added with connect! and come in three kinds:\n\nTransfer moves a uniform part of the contents: a volume, a mass, a fraction, or what is left (Transfer(), the default). The ingredients it brings may have a timing: Transfer(25u\"g\"; remaining = 10u\"minute\") for a hop with 10 minutes of boil left.\nDrain moves the liquid only, leaving behind the solids, the liquid the grain absorbs and the liquid the vessel retains.\nTransformations (MashRest, Boil, Chill, Ferment, Prime, Carbonate) turn all the contents of a node into the next stage of the vessel. They act on what is in the node: the grain mashed in, each hop for its own time, the culture pitched, the priming sugar. A transformation is the only arrow leaving its node.\n\nSeveral arrows may join the same nodes, e.g. two additions from the same lot of hops. Arrows that would create a cycle are refused.","title":"Arrows"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Brewing","page":"Concepts","text":"brew computes all the nodes in topological order and returns a BrewResult: res[label] is the contents of a node, and res[source => destination] what its arrows did (ArrowResults). What no arrow takes from a node is its leftover, and what the arrows release from the quiver (evaporated water, CO₂) is released. Over the whole quiver,\n\nsum textinitial contents = sum textleftovers + textreleased\n\nwhich the tests check for every brew. The products are the nodes nothing leaves.\n\nAfter a change of a node or an arrow, rebrew! recomputes only what is downstream of it.","title":"Brewing"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Models","page":"Concepts","text":"The derived quantities depend on models of BrewingFormulas: the conversion between gravity and extract, hop utilization, color, ABV. The defaults are DEFAULT_MODELS; a brew can choose others:\n\nres = brew(q; models = (bitterness = Rager(), color = Daniels()))\n\nThe results then compute with the same models (res.models).","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Calibration","page":"Concepts","text":"Real brewhouses do not convert all the potential extract. A MashRest has a conversion (0.95 by default), and the losses of the quiver (absorption, dead spaces, trub) follow from its arrows. A BeerJSON recipe can be calibrated to its stated brewhouse efficiency with BrewhouseEfficiency; see A BeerJSON recipe.","title":"Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/provenance/#Provenance-and-export","page":"Provenance and export","text":"","title":"Provenance and export"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/provenance/#Shares-and-attribution","page":"Provenance and export","text":"shares(res, q, product) gives the share of each lot (ingredient or water) used for a product:\n\nshares(res, q, :bottles)\nDict{Symbol, Float64} with 7 entries:\n  :sparge_water  => 1.0\n  :strike_water  => 1.0\n  :pale          => 1.0\n  :crystal       => 1.0\n  :priming_sugar => 1.0\n  :goldings      => 1.0\n  :yeast         => 1.0\n\nIn a single batch everything goes to the one product, so all the shares are 1; in a parti-gyle they split (see A parti-gyle).\n\nattribution(res, q, product) gives the share of every node: the fraction of its contents that goes into making the product, following the liquor, with what is left behind (spent grain, trub, yeast cake) allocated to the products it serves.\n\nattribution(res, q, :bottles)\nDict{Symbol, Float64} with 17 entries:\n  :grain_bed     => 1.0\n  :mash          => 1.0\n  :priming_sugar => 1.0\n  :beer          => 1.0\n  :goldings      => 1.0\n  :sparge_water  => 1.0\n  :strike_water  => 1.0\n  :pale          => 1.0\n  :chilled       => 1.0\n  :wort          => 1.0\n  :bottling      => 1.0\n  :bottles       => 1.0\n  :crystal       => 1.0\n  :fermentor     => 1.0\n  :rested        => 1.0\n  :yeast         => 1.0\n  :kettle        => 1.0\n\nIn a parti-gyle, the shares of each lot among the beers add up to what was used of it, which is how a quiver turns into one recipe per beer.","title":"Shares and attribution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/provenance/#BeerJSON-recipes","page":"Provenance and export","text":"BeerJSON recipes are linear, so a quiver is exported as one recipe per product, holding the shares of its lots: recipe(res, q, product), recipes(res, q) for all the products, and writejson(path, res, q) for a BeerJSON document.\n\nr = recipe(res, q, :bottles; name = \"Bitter\", author = \"BrewQuiver\")\n(r.name, quantity(r.batch_size), quantity(r.original_gravity))\n(\"Bitter\", 22.04202369904633 L, 1.0536099513756245 sg)\n\npath = writejson(tempname() * \".json\", res, q; author = \"BrewQuiver\")\nlength(readjson(BeerJSONParser, path).beerjson.recipes)\n1\n\nThe recipe has the mash steps, the boil (with its hop stand), the fermentation steps, the additions with their timing, the water and the efficiency of the brew (brewhouse_efficiency). A recipe exported from a quiver and imported back (with the equipment of its kettle) brews to the same wort and beer.","title":"BeerJSON recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/library/#Ingredient-library","page":"Ingredient library","text":"A Library holds ingredients by name, with what is in stock. It is loaded from the fermentable, hop and culture records of BeerJSON files (with their inventory), or built with push!:\n\nlib = Library()\npush!(lib, pale; stock = 10u\"kg\")\npush!(lib, crystal; stock = 0.2u\"kg\")\npush!(lib, goldings; stock = 100u\"g\")\npush!(lib, ale; stock = 2)\npush!(lib, dextrose; stock = 1u\"kg\")\nLibrary with 5 ingredients\n  Fermentable(\"Corn sugar\"), in stock 1.0 kg\n  Fermentable(\"Crystal 60\"), in stock 200.0 g\n  Hop(\"East Kent Goldings\"), in stock 100.0 g\n  Culture(\"English ale\"), in stock 2 ×\n  Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"), in stock 10.0 kg\n\nIngredients are found by name, or by part of it:\n\n(lib[\"Pale ale malt\"], lookup(lib, \"crystal\"))\n(Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"), [\"Crystal 60\"])","title":"Ingredient library"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/library/#Inventory","page":"Ingredient library","text":"res = brew(q)\nrequirements(res, q)          # what the brew uses of each ingredient\nDict{String, Any} with 5 entries:\n  \"East Kent Goldings\" => 0.05 kg\n  \"Pale ale malt\"      => 4.5 kg\n  \"English ale\"        => 1.0\n  \"Crystal 60\"         => 0.3 kg\n  \"Corn sugar\"         => 0.11 kg\n\nshortages(lib, res, q)        # what the stock lacks for it\n1-element Vector{@NamedTuple{name::String, needed, available}}:\n @NamedTuple{name::String, needed, available}((\"Crystal 60\", 0.3 kg, 0.2 kg))\n\nconsume! takes what the brew uses from the stock (warning about what is short), and stock! sets it:\n\nstock!(lib, \"Crystal 60\", 1u\"kg\")\nconsume!(lib, res, q)\nstock(lib, \"Pale ale malt\")\n5.5 kg","title":"Inventory"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/library/#Files","page":"Ingredient library","text":"lib = Library(\"my_ingredients.json\", \"more.json\")   # BeerJSON documents\nlib = readjson(Library, \"my_ingredients.json\")\nmerge!(lib, other)\nwritejson(\"my_ingredients.json\", lib)\n\nQuiver(recipe; library = lib) completes the additions of a recipe with the records of the library of the same name: the moisture of fermentables, and the attenuation of cultures.","title":"Files"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#A-parti-gyle","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"In a parti-gyle, the successive runnings of the same mash make worts of decreasing strength, which are boiled, fermented and often blended into several beers. It is a quiver that no linear recipe can describe: streams split at every drain and merge at every copper and every blend.\n\nThis tutorial follows the joint production of Fuller's Barley Wine, ESB, Pale Ale and Bitter (figure 1 of the BrewQuiver notes): two mashes are drained three times each, the i-th runnings of both go to the i-th copper, and the three fermented beers are racked and blended into four, aiming at the strengths of Golden Pride (OG about 1.088), ESB (1.059), London Pride (1.047) and Chiswick Bitter (1.035). The amounts are illustrative, not Fuller's actual recipes.","title":"A parti-gyle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Ingredients-and-vessels","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"using Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BrewQuiver\nusing BeerJSON: quantity\n\npale     = Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"; yield = 80u\"percent\", color = 3u\"°L\")\ncrystal  = Fermentable(\"Crystal 60\"; yield = 74u\"percent\", color = 60u\"°L\")\ninvert   = Fermentable(\"Invert sugar\"; kind = :sugar, yield = 100u\"percent\", color = 1u\"SRM\",\n                       moisture = 0)\ngoldings = Hop(\"East Kent Goldings\"; alpha = 5u\"percent\")\nale      = Culture(\"English ale\"; attenuation = 72u\"percent\")\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :goldings, goldings => 1.5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :invert, invert => 4u\"kg\")\n\n# three coppers, three fermenting vessels and three racking tanks\nfor i in 1:3\n    add!(q, Symbol(:yeast, i), ale => 1)\n    add!(q, Symbol(:kettle, i), Symbol(:wort, i), Symbol(:chilled, i); vessel = Symbol(:copper, i))\n    add!(q, Symbol(:fermentor, i), Symbol(:beer, i); vessel = Symbol(:fv, i))\n    add!(q, Symbol(:bright, i); vessel = Symbol(:tank, i))\nend","title":"Ingredients and vessels"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Two-mashes,-three-runnings-each","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"Each mash is drained three times: the first runnings from the mash itself, the second and third after sparging the grain bed. The i-th runnings go to the i-th copper, so each copper gets the runnings of both mashes.\n\nfor (m, grist) in enumerate(([pale => 70u\"kg\", crystal => 4u\"kg\"],\n                             [pale => 60u\"kg\", crystal => 2u\"kg\"]))\n    mash = Symbol(:mash, m)\n    beds = [Symbol(:bed, m, :_, i) for i in 1:3]\n    lots = [Symbol(:grist, m, :_, j) for j in eachindex(grist)]\n    foreach((l, g) -> add!(q, l, g), lots, grist)\n    add!(q, Symbol(:strike, m), water(190u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\"))\n    add!(q, mash, beds...; vessel = Symbol(:mashtun, m))\n    connect!(q, [Symbol(:strike, m); lots] => mash)\n    connect!(q, mash => beds[1], MashRest(temperature = 66u\"°C\", time = 60u\"minute\"))\n    for (i, v) in enumerate((nothing, 60u\"L\", 40u\"L\"))\n        if v !== nothing                                     # sparge the grain bed\n            add!(q, Symbol(:sparge, m, :_, i), water(v; temperature = 80u\"°C\"))\n            connect!(q, beds[i - 1] => beds[i])               # the grain and what it holds\n            connect!(q, Symbol(:sparge, m, :_, i) => beds[i])\n        end\n        connect!(q, beds[i] => Symbol(:kettle, i), Drain(absorption = 1.0u\"L/kg\"))\n    end\nend","title":"Two mashes, three runnings each"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Boils,-fermentations-and-racking","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"The strongest wort gets the invert sugar and most of the hops:\n\nfor (i, hops) in enumerate((1100u\"g\", 270u\"g\", 130u\"g\"))\n    kettle, wort, chilled, fv, beer, bright =\n        (Symbol(x, i) for x in (:kettle, :wort, :chilled, :fermentor, :beer, :bright))\n    connect!(q, :goldings => kettle, Transfer(hops; remaining = 90u\"minute\"))\n    i == 1 && connect!(q, :invert => kettle)\n    connect!(q, kettle => wort, Boil(time = 90u\"minute\", evaporation = 8u\"L/hr\"))\n    connect!(q, wort => chilled, Chill(temperature = 18u\"°C\"))\n    connect!(q, chilled => fv, Drain(retain = 2u\"L\"))\n    connect!(q, Symbol(:yeast, i) => fv)\n    connect!(q, fv => beer, Ferment(temperature = 19u\"°C\", time = 7u\"d\"))\n    connect!(q, beer => bright, Drain(retain = 2u\"L\"))\nend","title":"Boils, fermentations and racking"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Blending","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"Four beers from three: the blends are just nodes where transfers merge. A Transfer of a percentage takes that fraction; a plain Transfer() takes the rest.\n\nadd!(q, :barley_wine, :esb, :pale_ale, :bitter)\nconnect!(q, :bright1 => :esb, Transfer(6u\"percent\"))\nconnect!(q, :bright1 => :barley_wine)\nconnect!(q, :bright2 => :esb, Transfer(60u\"percent\"))\nconnect!(q, :bright2 => :pale_ale)\nconnect!(q, :bright3 => :pale_ale, Transfer(16u\"percent\"))\nconnect!(q, :bright3 => :bitter)","title":"Blending"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#The-beers","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"res = brew(q)\nbeers = (:barley_wine, :esb, :pale_ale, :bitter)\nfor b in beers\n    println(rpad(b, 12), round(u\"L\", volume(res[b])), \"  OG \", round(u\"sg\", original_gravity(res[b]); digits = 3),\n            \"  \", round(u\"percent\", abv(res[b]); digits = 1), \" ABV  \", round(u\"IBU\", ibu(res[b])))\nend\nbarley_wine 277.0 L  OG 1.093 sg  9.2 % ABV  35.0 IBU\nesb         80.0 L  OG 1.06 sg  5.8 % ABV  35.0 IBU\npale_ale    52.0 L  OG 1.047 sg  4.6 % ABV  34.0 IBU\nbitter      54.0 L  OG 1.034 sg  3.2 % ABV  31.0 IBU\n\nThe stages each vessel goes through:\n\nsort!(collect(vessels(q)))\n11-element Vector{Pair{Symbol, Vector{Symbol}}}:\n  :copper1 => [:kettle1, :wort1, :chilled1]\n  :copper2 => [:kettle2, :wort2, :chilled2]\n  :copper3 => [:kettle3, :wort3, :chilled3]\n      :fv1 => [:fermentor1, :beer1]\n      :fv2 => [:fermentor2, :beer2]\n      :fv3 => [:fermentor3, :beer3]\n :mashtun1 => [:mash1, :bed1_1, :bed1_2, :bed1_3]\n :mashtun2 => [:mash2, :bed2_1, :bed2_2, :bed2_3]\n    :tank1 => [:bright1]\n    :tank2 => [:bright2]\n    :tank3 => [:bright3]","title":"The beers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#A-change-upstream","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"A more attenuative fermentation of the second gyle changes the ESB and the Pale Ale, which contain it, but not the Barley Wine or the Bitter. rebrew! recomputes only the nodes downstream:\n\nq[:fermentor2 => :beer2] = Ferment(temperature = 19u\"°C\", time = 7u\"d\", attenuation = 80u\"percent\")\nrebrew!(res, q, :fermentor2)\nfor b in beers\n    println(rpad(b, 12), round(u\"percent\", abv(res[b]); digits = 2), \" ABV\")\nend\nbarley_wine 9.23 % ABV\nesb         6.25 % ABV\npale_ale    4.97 % ABV\nbitter      3.23 % ABV","title":"A change upstream"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Which-lots-made-the-ESB","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"shares follows the liquor back to the lots: the fraction of each lot used for a product. The shares of each lot among the four beers add up to what was used of it.\n\nsort!([l => round(s; digits = 3) for (l, s) in shares(res, q, :esb)]; by = first)\n12-element Vector{Pair{Symbol, Float64}}:\n  :goldings => 0.152\n  :grist1_1 => 0.128\n  :grist1_2 => 0.128\n  :grist2_1 => 0.126\n  :grist2_2 => 0.126\n    :invert => 0.06\n :sparge1_2 => 0.269\n :sparge2_2 => 0.295\n   :strike1 => 0.128\n   :strike2 => 0.126\n    :yeast1 => 0.06\n    :yeast2 => 0.6","title":"Which lots made the ESB"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#Blending-to-a-target","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"The blend fractions can be solved for targets, e.g. the original gravities of the ESB and the Pale Ale (see Targets and solving):\n\nsol = solve!(q, :esb => original_gravity => 1.059u\"sg\", :pale_ale => original_gravity => 1.047u\"sg\";\n             vary = [Setting(:bright2 => :esb, :amount), Setting(:bright3 => :pale_ale, :amount)])\nSolution in 3 iterations\n  :bright2 → :esb amount: 0.6663813559104544\n  :bright3 → :pale_ale amount: 0.13722467563691831\n  original_gravity(:esb) = 1.0590000000130813 sg (goal 1.059 sg)\n  original_gravity(:pale_ale) = 1.0469999999827806 sg (goal 1.047 sg)","title":"Blending to a target"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/partigyle/#As-BeerJSON-recipes","page":"A parti-gyle","text":"BeerJSON recipes are linear, so the quiver exports as one recipe per beer, each with its shares of the lots (see Provenance and export):\n\n[r.name => quantity(r.batch_size) for r in recipes(brew(q), q; products = collect(beers))]\n4-element Vector{Pair{String, Quantity{Float64, 𝐋^3, Unitful.FreeUnits{(L,), 𝐋^3, nothing}}}}:\n \"barley_wine\" => 281.1093579699416 L\n         \"esb\" => 88.55150589587589 L\n    \"pale_ale\" => 44.40451455434212 L\n      \"bitter\" => 56.931614590226395 L","title":"As BeerJSON recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#A-BeerJSON-recipe","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"BeerJSON is the JSON format for brewing recipes, read and written in Julia by BeerJSON.jl. A BeerJSON recipe builds its quiver: its ingredients as lots, the water, and the stages of the mash tun, the kettle, the fermentor and the packaging.","title":"A BeerJSON recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#Reading-a-recipe","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"We take the RR Summer Bitter from the samples of BeerJSON.jl:\n\nusing Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BeerJSON, BrewQuiver\n\nfile = joinpath(pkgdir(BeerJSON), \"test\", \"samples\", \"converted\", \"RRSummerBitter.json\")\nrecipe = only(readjson(BeerJSONParser, file).beerjson.recipes)\nrecipe.name\n\"RR Summer Bitter\"","title":"Reading a recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#Its-quiver","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"q = Quiver(recipe; calibration = BrewhouseEfficiency())\nQuiver with 17 nodes and 18 arrows\n  lots\n    :pale_malt_maris_otter  7.0 kg Pale Malt, Maris Otter\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :cara_50  1.0 kg Cara 50\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :target  40.0 g Target\n        Transfer(all, 60.0 min remaining) → :kettle\n    :goldings_east_kent  30.0 g Goldings, East Kent\n        Transfer(15.0 g, 30.0 min remaining) → :kettle\n        Transfer(15.0 g, 15.0 min remaining) → :kettle\n    :windsor_yeast  1 × Windsor Yeast\n        Transfer(all) → :fermentor\n    :strike_water  20.86 L, 1.0 sg, 68.9 °C\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :sparge_water_1  29.36 L, 1.0 sg, 75.6 °C\n        Transfer(all) → :grain_bed_1\n  [mashtun]\n    :mash\n        MashRest(68.9 °C, 40.0 min) → :mash_1\n    :mash_1\n        MashRest(75.6 °C, 10.0 min) → :mash_2\n    :mash_2\n        Transfer(rest) → :grain_bed_1\n        Drain() → :kettle\n    :grain_bed_1\n        Drain() → :kettle\n  [kettle]  Vessel(\"Kettle\"; 0.0 L dead space, evaporating 5.64 L/h)\n    :kettle\n        Boil(60.0 min) → :wort\n    :wort\n        Chill(20.0 °C) → :chilled\n    :chilled\n        Drain() → :fermentor\n  [fermentor]\n    :fermentor\n        Ferment(20.0 °C, 14.0 d) → :beer\n    :beer\n        Drain() → :packaging\n  [package]\n    :packaging\n\nEach ingredient became a lot, with an arrow per addition, timed as in the recipe; the mash steps became mash rests (and infusions, decoctions), the boil a Boil, the fermentation steps Ferments. The ingredient records convert to the working types with Fermentable(addition), Hop(addition) and Culture(addition).\n\nThe volumes a recipe does not give are solved for its own targets: the sparge water for its pre-boil volume, the evaporation rate and the kettle loss for its batch size:\n\nvolume(q[:sparge_water_1].initial)\n29.358896083444318 L\n\nAn equipment (a BeerJSON EquipmentType, given as Quiver(recipe; equipment = ...) or taken from the document) gives the grain absorption, the dead spaces, the losses and the boil rate, as Vessels of the quiver.","title":"Its quiver"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#Calibration","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"A recipe states the brewhouse efficiency of the brewery it was written for. With BrewhouseEfficiency, the conversion of the mash is adjusted so that the quiver reproduces it; with NoCalibration (the default), the efficiency follows from the processes of the quiver:\n\nres = brew(q)\n(brewhouse_efficiency(res, q), quantity(recipe.efficiency.brewhouse))\n(0.7499999995992039, 75.0 %)\n\nq0 = Quiver(recipe)\nbrewhouse_efficiency(brew(q0), q0)\n0.8834669890267864","title":"Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#The-beer","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"beer = res[:beer]\n(og = original_gravity(beer), fg = gravity(beer), abv = abv(beer), ibu = ibu(beer), color = srm(beer))\n(og = 1.0450489739696107 sg, fg = 1.0109539531301333 sg, abv = 4.521811823604397 %, ibu = 36.503927217093114 IBU, color = 6.749726486346211 SRM)\n\nWhen a recipe states its own estimates (original_gravity, ibu_estimate, color_estimate), they may differ from these: the recipe's software may use other formulas for the hop utilization or the color, and its losses are not always stated. Choose the models with brew(q; models = ...).","title":"The beer"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#What-the-importer-reads","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"Fermentable, hop, culture and miscellaneous additions, with their timing (mash, boil, first wort, whirlpool, dry hop, priming).\nThe water additions, as the WaterProfile of the water, and the \"water agent\" additions (gypsum, calcium chloride, lactic acid, ...) as Salts and Acids; the distilled water pH of fermentable records.\nThe mash steps: infusions (the water that reaches their temperature, when they give no amount), temperature steps and decoctions (their boil and return).\nThe boil steps, a whirlpool as a hop stand, the chilling.\nThe fermentation steps, and priming as a priming sugar.\n\nWhere a recipe is incomplete (a culture without attenuation, a dry hop without time), the importer warns and takes a default. A Library can complete it: Quiver(recipe; library = lib) (see Ingredient library).","title":"What the importer reads"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beerjson/#And-back","page":"A BeerJSON recipe","text":"A quiver exports as BeerJSON recipes, one per product (see Provenance and export). A recipe exported from a quiver and imported back, with the equipment of its kettle, brews to the same wort and beer.","title":"And back"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/solving/#Targets-and-solving","page":"Targets and solving","text":"solve! changes parameters of a quiver so that its brew meets targets, by Newton's method on its brews.","title":"Targets and solving"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/solving/#Targets","page":"Targets and solving","text":"A target is node => f => goal, for any function f of the contents of the node (gravity, original_gravity, volume, ibu, srm, abv, carbonation, ...), with the goal in any of its units: a gravity in °P is converted with the gravity model.","title":"Targets"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/solving/#What-varies","page":"Targets and solving","text":"Amount(lots...) scales the amounts of lots together, keeping their proportions (a grain bill), with the transfers of masses and volumes leaving them.\nSetting(source => destination, field) varies a numeric field of an arrow: the amount of a transfer (a blend fraction), the time of a boil, the stand of a hop stand, the temperature of a mash rest, ...\n\nThere are as many parameters as targets.","title":"What varies"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/solving/#Example","page":"Targets and solving","text":"The grain bill, hops, sparge water and priming sugar for an original gravity, a bitterness, a pre-boil volume and a carbonation:\n\nsol = solve!(q, :fermentor => gravity => 1.052u\"sg\", :wort => ibu => 35u\"IBU\",\n             :kettle => volume => 28u\"L\", :bottles => carbonation => 2.4u\"vols\";\n             vary = [Amount(:pale, :crystal), Amount(:goldings), Amount(:sparge_water),\n                     Amount(:priming_sugar)])\nSolution in 3 iterations\n  amount of :pale, :crystal: :crystal Contents(302.0 g Crystal 60), :pale Contents(4.531 kg Pale ale malt)\n  amount of :goldings: Contents(87.5 g East Kent Goldings)\n  amount of :sparge_water: Contents(14.98 L, 1.0 sg, 80.0 °C)\n  amount of :priming_sugar: Contents(151.0 g Corn sugar)\n  gravity(:fermentor) = 1.0519999999999998 sg (goal 1.052 sg)\n  ibu(:wort) = 35.000000000014815 IBU (goal 35 IBU)\n  volume(:kettle) = 27.999999999999996 L (goal 28 L)\n  carbonation(:bottles) = 2.399999999999974 vols (goal 2.4 vols)\n\nThe quiver is changed in place:\n\nq[:pale].initial\nSolids: 4.531 kg Pale ale malt\n\nA goal in other units:\n\nsolve!(q, :fermentor => gravity => 13u\"°P\"; vary = [Amount(:pale, :crystal)])\nq[:pale].initial\nSolids: 4.583 kg Pale ale malt\n\nThe Solution holds the iterations, the parameters and the values reached. If the targets cannot be met (e.g. a gravity that would need a negative amount), solve! throws an error, leaving the quiver as it was.\n\nThe blend fractions of a parti-gyle are solved the same way; see A parti-gyle.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2:2","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Liquors","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Liquors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Water-chemistry","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Water chemistry"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Ingredients","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Ingredients"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Contents-and-solids","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Contents and solids"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Arrows","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Arrows"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Quivers","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Quivers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Equipment","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Equipment"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Brewing","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Brewing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Timeline","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Timeline"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Targets-and-solving","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Targets and solving"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Provenance","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#BeerJSON","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"BeerJSON"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Files","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Ingredient-library","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Ingredient library"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.BrewQuiver","page":"API reference","text":"BrewQuiver\n\nThe engine of the BrewQuiver brewing software: the brewing process as a quiver, a directed acyclic multigraph whose nodes are the Contents of vessels at the stages of the brewing (the lots of ingredients, the water, the mash, the wort in the kettle, the beer in the fermentor, ...) and whose arrows are the processes between them: transfers, drains and transformations (mash rests, boils, fermentations, ...). Streams can be split and merged, as in parti-gyle brewing or blending.\n\nThe state of each node holds conserved quantities (a Liquor and solids), so that mixing and splitting are exact; gravity, alcohol, bitterness and color are computed with BrewingFormulas.jl.\n\nusing Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BrewQuiver\n\npale = Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"; yield = 80u\"percent\", color = 3u\"°L\")\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :pale, pale => 5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :strike_water, water(16u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :mash, :rested; vessel = :mashtun)\nconnect!(q, [:strike_water, :pale] => :mash)\nconnect!(q, :mash => :rested, MashRest(temperature = 67u\"°C\"))\n...\nres = brew(q)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.BrewQuiver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Liquor","page":"API reference","text":"Liquor(; water = 0u\"kg\", extract = 0u\"kg\", alcohol = 0u\"kg\", isoalpha = 0u\"mg\",\n         color = 0.0, co2 = 0u\"g\", temperature = 20u\"°C\", minerals = Minerals())\n\nThe state of a liquid (water, wort, beer, ...), given by its conserved quantities:\n\nwater: mass of water (a volume is taken at 20 °C);\nextract: mass of dissolved (real) extract;\nalcohol: mass of ethanol;\nisoalpha: mass of iso-alpha acids;\ncolor: color units, as lb × °L (malt color units times volume in gal);\nco2: mass of dissolved CO₂;\ntemperature;\nminerals: the Minerals dissolved.\n\nLiquors are mixed with + and split with * by a fraction. Derived quantities: volume, gravity, original_gravity, real_extract, abw, abv, ibu, srm, carbonation. See also water and wort.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Liquor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.water","page":"API reference","text":"water(volume; temperature = 20u\"°C\", profile = nothing)\n\nA liquor of water with the given volume at 20 °C, pure or with the ions of a WaterProfile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.water"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.wort","page":"API reference","text":"wort(volume, gravity; temperature = 20u\"°C\", eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nA liquor of unfermented wort with the given volume, at 20 °C, and gravity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.wort"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.mass","page":"API reference","text":"mass(liquor)\n\nThe total mass of the liquor: water, extract and alcohol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.mass"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.volume","page":"API reference","text":"volume(liquor; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe total volume of the liquor at 20 °C: its mass divided by its density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.volume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.gravity","page":"API reference","text":"gravity(liquor; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe (apparent) specific gravity of the liquor, in sg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.original_gravity","page":"API reference","text":"original_gravity(liquor; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe original gravity of the liquor, in sg: the gravity it had before any fermentation, by Balling's relations. For a blend, the original gravity of the blend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.original_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.carbonation","page":"API reference","text":"carbonation(liquor; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe dissolved CO₂ of the liquor, in vols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.carbonation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.with_temperature","page":"API reference","text":"with_temperature(liquor, temperature)\n\nThe liquor at another temperature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.with_temperature"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Minerals","page":"API reference","text":"Minerals(; calcium = 0u\"mg\", magnesium = 0u\"mg\", sodium = 0u\"mg\", chloride = 0u\"mg\",\n           sulfate = 0u\"mg\", alkalinity = 0u\"mg\", other = 0u\"mg\")\n\nThe minerals dissolved in a liquor, as conserved quantities: the masses of calcium, magnesium, sodium, chloride and sulfate, the alkalinity as a mass of CaCO₃ (the equivalents of bicarbonate, carbonate and hydroxide, less those of the acids added; it can be negative), and the mass of the other species dissolved (bicarbonate, carbonate, hydroxide, acids, water of crystallization), so that the mass of the salts and acids added is conserved. They are mixed with + and split with * by a fraction, with the liquor. See WaterProfile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Minerals"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.WaterProfile","page":"API reference","text":"WaterProfile(; calcium = 0u\"mg/L\", magnesium = 0u\"mg/L\", sodium = 0u\"mg/L\",\n               chloride = 0u\"mg/L\", sulfate = 0u\"mg/L\", bicarbonate = 0u\"mg/L\")\n\nThe concentrations of the main ions of a water (in mg/L or ppm), e.g. a water report or a classic brewing water. See water and profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.WaterProfile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.profile","page":"API reference","text":"profile(liquor), profile(contents)\n\nThe WaterProfile of the liquor: the concentrations of its minerals in its volume, with the alkalinity as bicarbonate (negative if acids took more than the alkalinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.profile"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BrewingFormulas.residual_alkalinity-Tuple{Liquor}","page":"API reference","text":"residual_alkalinity(liquor), residual_alkalinity(contents)\n\nKolbach's residual alkalinity of the water of the liquor, in mg/L as CaCO₃ (see BrewingFormulas' residual_alkalinity), from its minerals and volume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.residual_alkalinity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BrewingFormulas.mash_ph-Tuple{Contents}","page":"API reference","text":"mash_ph(contents)\n\nAn estimate of the pH of a mash with the contents of a node before its mash rest (the grain and the water, with the salts and acids added to it), by the balance of charges of BrewingFormulas' mash_ph: the distilled water pH and buffering of each grain (see Fermentable; pale base malt by default), and the residual alkalinity and volume of the liquor. Grains darker than 8 SRM without a distilled water pH are taken as base malt, with a warning, since crystal and roasted malts lower the pH much more.\n\nres = brew(q)\nmash_ph(res[:mash])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.mash_ph"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractIngredient","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractIngredient\n\nSupertype of the ingredients: Fermentable, Hop, Culture, Salt, Acid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractIngredient"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Fermentable","page":"API reference","text":"Fermentable(name; kind = :grain, yield = 80u\"percent\", color = 2u\"SRM\", moisture = 4u\"percent\",\n            di_ph = nothing, buffering = nothing)\nFermentable(name; potential = 1.037u\"sg\", ...)\n\nA fermentable ingredient. kind is one of the BeerJSON types (:grain, :extract, :dry_extract, :sugar, :fruit, :juice, :honey, :other); yield is the extract yield relative to sucrose, on dry basis, or it is given by the potential (the specific gravity of 1 lb in 1 US gallon); color is in any color unit. For the mash pH (see mash_ph), di_ph is the pH of a mash of the grain in distilled water and buffering its buffering capacity, in mEq/(kg pH), when known.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Fermentable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Hop","page":"API reference","text":"Hop(name; alpha, form = :pellet)\n\nA hop with alpha acid content alpha (a percentage or fraction) and form (:pellet, :leaf, :wet_leaf, :plug, :powder, :extract).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Hop"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Culture","page":"API reference","text":"Culture(name; attenuation = 75u\"percent\", kind = :ale, form = :liquid)\n\nA yeast or other culture with the given apparent attenuation (a percentage or fraction), of the kind and form of BeerJSON (kind one of :ale, :lager, :brett, :lacto, :kveik, ...; form one of :liquid, :dry, :slant, :culture, :dregs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Culture"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Salt","page":"API reference","text":"Salt(salt::AbstractSalt; name)\n\nA brewing salt (an AbstractSalt of BrewingFormulas: Gypsum(), CalciumChloride(), EpsomSalt(), ...). A lot of it, Salt(Gypsum()) => 5u\"g\", holds its ions dissolved (see Minerals), so that they mix with the water it is added to.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Salt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Acid","page":"API reference","text":"Acid(acid::AbstractAcid; name)\n\nAn acid solution (an AbstractAcid of BrewingFormulas: LacticAcid(), PhosphoricAcid()). A lot of it, Acid(LacticAcid()) => 2u\"mL\" (or a mass), holds its acid dissolved, lowering the alkalinity of the water it is added to by its milliequivalents at pH 5.4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Acid"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.HOP_FORM_FACTORS","page":"API reference","text":"HOP_FORM_FACTORS\n\nUtilization factors of the hop forms, relative to leaf hops: pellets and powders are taken to give 10% more bitterness. It can be modified to change the defaults.\n\nThe 10% for pellets is a homebrewing convention, not part of Tinseth's or Rager's models. The Python BrewQuiver divided Tinseth's utilization by 0.9 and used the factors 1 for pellets and 0.9 for leaf hops, i.e. pellets about 11% above leaf hops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.HOP_FORM_FACTORS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Contents","page":"API reference","text":"Contents(liquor = zero(Liquor), solids = [])\nContents(ingredient => amount)\n\nWhat is in a vessel at a stage of the brewing: a Liquor and solids (AbstractSolid). Contents are the nodes of a Quiver; they are mixed with + and split with * by a fraction.\n\nThe derived quantities of the liquor (volume, gravity, abv, ibu, srm, ...) are also available for the contents. See also total_mass and grist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Contents"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.liquor","page":"API reference","text":"liquor(contents), solids(contents)\n\nThe liquor and the solids of contents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.liquor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.total_mass","page":"API reference","text":"total_mass(contents)\n\nThe mass of everything in contents: the liquor (with its iso-alpha acids and CO₂) and the solids, except those given as counts. It is conserved by the quiver, up to what the transformations release (evaporated water, CO₂).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.total_mass"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.grist","page":"API reference","text":"grist(contents)\n\nThe mass of grain in contents, fresh or spent, which sets the liquid it absorbs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.grist"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractSolid","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractSolid\n\nSupertype of the solids in Contents: Portion, Residue, HopMatter, YeastCake.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractSolid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Portion","page":"API reference","text":"Portion(ingredient, amount; timing = nothing)\n\nAn amount of an ingredient as it was added: a mass, or a count (e.g. packs of yeast), with its AbstractTiming.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Portion"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Residue","page":"API reference","text":"Residue(mass, grist)\n\nThe insoluble residue of fermentables after their extract was dissolved: spent grain, with the mass grist of the grain it came from, which sets the liquid it absorbs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Residue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.HopMatter","page":"API reference","text":"HopMatter(mass)\n\nWhat remains of hops after a boil.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.HopMatter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.YeastCake","page":"API reference","text":"YeastCake(mass)\n\nThe yeast after a fermentation: what was pitched (when given as a mass) and what grew.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.YeastCake"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractTiming","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractTiming\n\nWhen an ingredient arrives in a node, relative to the transformation that follows it: Remaining or At. Without a timing, it is there from the start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractTiming"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Remaining","page":"API reference","text":"Remaining(time)\n\nArrival with time remaining in the next transformation, e.g. a hop added with 10 minutes of boil left, or a dry hop with 3 days of fermentation left.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Remaining"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.At","page":"API reference","text":"At(time)\n\nArrival time after the start of the next transformation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.At"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractArrow","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractArrow\n\nSupertype of the arrows of a Quiver: Transfer, Drain and the transformations (AbstractTransformation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractArrow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractTransformation","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractTransformation <: AbstractArrow\n\nSupertype of the arrows that turn all the contents of a node into the next state of the same vessel: MashRest, Boil, Chill, Ferment, Prime, Carbonate. A transformation must be the only arrow leaving its node. A new one implements transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractTransformation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.transform","page":"API reference","text":"transform(t::AbstractTransformation, contents::Contents, models) -> (contents, released)\n\nThe contents after the transformation t, and what it released from the quiver, as a NamedTuple (water, co2) of masses (negative for what it took in).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.transform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Transfer","page":"API reference","text":"Transfer(amount = nothing; remaining = nothing, at = nothing, duration = 0u\"minute\")\n\nMoves a uniform part of the contents of a node: a volume (of the liquor), a mass, a fraction (a percentage or a plain number from 0 to 1), or nothing for what is left by the other arrows (shared equally among the transfers of the rest).\n\nThe ingredients moved arrive with the timing remaining or at (see Remaining, At), e.g. Transfer(20u\"g\"; remaining = 10u\"minute\") for a hop with 10 minutes of boil left. The transfer takes the time duration (see timeline).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Transfer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Drain","page":"API reference","text":"Drain(; absorption = nothing, retain = nothing, duration = nothing, hop_absorption = nothing)\n\nMoves the liquid of a node, leaving behind its solids, the liquid absorbed by the grain (absorption per mass of grain, see grist), the liquid absorbed by the hops (hop_absorption per mass of hops, fresh or spent, e.g. after a dry hop) and the volume retain (dead space, trub, yeast cake). It acts on what the transfers of amounts leave, before the transfers of the rest; a node has at most one drain. Draining takes the time duration (see timeline).\n\nWhat is not given comes from the Vessel of the node, if it is equipped (see equip!): its grain absorption, its dead space, and its drain rate for the duration; or else 1.0 L/kg of grain absorption, no hop absorption, nothing retained and no time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Drain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.MashRest","page":"API reference","text":"MashRest(; temperature = 67u\"°C\", time = 60u\"minute\", conversion = 95u\"percent\")\n\nA mash rest: the grain in the node gives the fraction conversion of its potential extract (and its moisture and color) to the liquor, at temperature, leaving its Residue, the spent grain. Soluble fermentables dissolve completely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.MashRest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Boil","page":"API reference","text":"Boil(; time = 60u\"minute\", evaporation = nothing, stand = 0u\"minute\",\n       stand_temperature = 80u\"°C\")\n\nBoils the contents of a node for time, evaporating water at the rate evaporation (a volume, or a fraction of the volume, per time; if not given, the evaporation rate of the Vessel of the node, or else 4 L/h) and driving off the CO₂, followed by a hop stand (or whirlpool) of stand at stand_temperature. Soluble fermentables dissolve; each hop isomerizes during its own time in the boil (see AbstractTiming) and in the stand, with the utilization model models.bitterness at the mean of the gravities before and after the boil and the factor HOP_FORM_FACTORS of its form, leaving its HopMatter. A time in the stand counts as isomerization_factor(stand_temperature) times as much boil, by the isomerization rate of BrewingFormulas.\n\nThe timing of the hops counts from the boil: Remaining is the time left in the boil, before the stand, and At a time from the start of the boil, so that a hop added At(time + 10u\"minute\") stands for the last stand - 10u\"minute\". The boil and the stand take time + stand (see duration), and the wort is left at stand_temperature after a stand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Boil"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Chill","page":"API reference","text":"Chill(; temperature = 20u\"°C\", time = 0u\"minute\")\n\nBrings the contents of a node to temperature, e.g. the wort after the boil, before it is transferred to the fermentor. Nothing else changes (the volumes of the quiver are at 20 °C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Chill"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Ferment","page":"API reference","text":"Ferment(; temperature = 20u\"°C\", time = 14u\"d\", attenuation = nothing)\n\nFerments the contents of a node with the cultures in it, down to the apparent attenuation of its original extract: by default, the largest attenuation of the cultures present. Soluble fermentables dissolve first.\n\nBy Balling, each 2.0665 g of extract give 1 g of ethanol, 0.9565 g of CO₂ and 0.11 g of yeast, which joins the YeastCake. The CO₂ escapes except for the residual CO₂ at temperature (see residual_co2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Ferment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Prime","page":"API reference","text":"Prime(; sugar = CornSugar(), time = 14u\"d\")\n\nBottle or keg conditioning: the soluble fermentables in the node (the priming sugar) are fully fermented, and their CO₂ stays dissolved. The CO₂ yield is that of sugar, an AbstractPrimingSugar of BrewingFormulas; equal numbers of moles of ethanol and CO₂ are produced, and the water balances the masses (the water of a monohydrate is released, the water of the hydrolysis of sucrose is taken). See priming_mass for the mass of sugar giving a carbonation level. The conditioning takes the given time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Prime"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Carbonate","page":"API reference","text":"Carbonate(carbonation; time = 2u\"d\")\n\nForced carbonation of the contents of a node to the carbonation level, with CO₂ from outside the quiver (released as a negative mass), taking the given time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Carbonate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.priming_mass","page":"API reference","text":"priming_mass(contents, carbonation; sugar = CornSugar(), eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe mass of priming sugar that Prime needs to bring contents to the carbonation level, accounting for the CO₂ already dissolved and for the change of volume due to the ethanol and water produced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.priming_mass"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Quiver","page":"API reference","text":"Quiver()\n\nAn empty quiver: the brewing process as a directed acyclic graph whose nodes, labelled by symbols, are the Contents of vessels at the stages of the brewing, and whose arrows (AbstractArrow) are the processes between them: transfers, drains and transformations. Several arrows may join the same two nodes.\n\nBuild it with add! and connect!, and compute it with brew.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Quiver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Node","page":"API reference","text":"Node\n\nA node of a Quiver: its initial contents (the lots on the shelf, the water; empty for the stages filled by arrows), the vessel it is in, if given, and view, free data for interfaces (a position, a color, ...; see nodeview), kept by the files of quivers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Node"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.add!","page":"API reference","text":"add!(q::Quiver, label, initial; vessel = nothing)\nadd!(q::Quiver, labels::Symbol...; vessel = nothing)\n\nAdds to q a node with initial contents (a lot: a liquor such as water(16u\"L\"), or ingredient => amount), or empty nodes, to be filled by arrows, with the given labels. They are in vessel, if given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.add!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.connect!","page":"API reference","text":"connect!(q::Quiver, source => destination, arrow = Transfer())\nconnect!(q::Quiver, [sources...] => destination, arrow = Transfer())\n\nAdds an arrow (a Transfer, Drain or transformation) from the node source to the node destination, e.g.\n\nconnect!(q, [:strike_water, :pale] => :mash)                  # transfer everything\nconnect!(q, :goldings => :kettle, Transfer(20u\"g\"; remaining = 10u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :kettle => :wort, Boil(time = 60u\"minute\"))\n\nArrows that would create a cycle are refused, keeping q acyclic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.connect!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.disconnect!","page":"API reference","text":"disconnect!(q::Quiver, source => destination)\ndisconnect!(q::Quiver, source => destination, i)\n\nRemoves all the arrows from source to destination, or only the i-th of them, keeping the others in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.disconnect!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.remove!","page":"API reference","text":"remove!(q::Quiver, label)\n\nRemoves the node label and its arrows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.remove!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.rename!","page":"API reference","text":"rename!(q::Quiver, old, new)\n\nRenames the node old to new, keeping its contents, vessel, view and arrows, and its place in the order of the nodes and of the arrows (so that renaming it back gives the same quiver, and the same file). Brew again afterwards (rebrew!(res, q, new) will do).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.rename!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{Quiver, Any, Symbol}","page":"API reference","text":"q[label] = initial\n\nReplaces the initial contents of the node label, keeping its vessel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{Quiver, AbstractArrow, Pair{Symbol, Symbol}}","page":"API reference","text":"q[source => destination] = arrow\n\nReplaces the arrows from source to destination by arrow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{Quiver, Pair{Symbol, Symbol}, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"q[source => destination, i]\n\nThe i-th of the arrows from source to destination, in the order they were connected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{Quiver, AbstractArrow, Pair{Symbol, Symbol}, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"q[source => destination, i] = arrow\n\nReplaces the i-th of the arrows from source to destination by arrow, keeping the others in order (e.g. the second of two hop additions from the same lot).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.nodes","page":"API reference","text":"nodes(q::Quiver)\n\nThe labels of the nodes of q, in topological order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.nodes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.arrows","page":"API reference","text":"arrows(q::Quiver)\n\nAll the arrows of q, as a vector of (source, destination, arrow).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.arrows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.vessel","page":"API reference","text":"vessel(q::Quiver, label)\n\nThe vessel of the node label, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.vessel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.vessels","page":"API reference","text":"vessels(q::Quiver)\n\nThe nodes of each vessel, in topological order: the stages the vessel goes through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.vessels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.predecessors","page":"API reference","text":"predecessors(q::Quiver, label), successors(q::Quiver, label)\n\nThe labels of the nodes with arrows to, or from, the node label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.predecessors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.descendants","page":"API reference","text":"descendants(q::Quiver, label)\n\nThe labels of all the nodes downstream of label, including itself, in topological order: those to recompute when label changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.descendants"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.nodeview","page":"API reference","text":"nodeview(q::Quiver, label) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nThe free data of the node label for interfaces (a position, a color, ...), which BrewQuiver does not use but keeps in the files of quivers (see writejson). It can be changed in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.nodeview"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Vessel","page":"API reference","text":"Vessel(; name = \"\", capacity = nothing, dead_space = nothing, absorption = nothing,\n       hop_absorption = nothing, evaporation = nothing, drain_rate = nothing)\n\nThe equipment of a vessel: its capacity, its dead_space (the liquid left behind when it is drained: the dead space under a false bottom, the trub of a kettle, the yeast cake of a fermentor), the grain absorption per mass of grain (a mash or lauter tun), the hop_absorption per mass of hops (a kettle, a fermentor with dry hops), the evaporation rate of a boil (a volume, or a fraction of the volume, per time) and the drain_rate (a volume per time). Attach it to the nodes of a vessel with equip!; what is not given is left to the arrows or their defaults.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Vessel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.equip!","page":"API reference","text":"equip!(q::Quiver, vessel::Symbol, equipment::Vessel)\n\nAttaches the equipment to the vessel of q: the arrows leaving the nodes in that vessel take from it what they do not give themselves (a Drain its absorption, dead space and drain rate, a Boil its evaporation rate), and its capacity is checked by overfilled. After a change of equipment, brew again.\n\nequip!(q, :kettle, Vessel(capacity = 40u\"L\", dead_space = 1.5u\"L\", evaporation = 4u\"L/hr\",\n                          drain_rate = 1u\"L/minute\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.equip!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.equipment","page":"API reference","text":"equipment(q::Quiver, vessel::Symbol)\n\nThe Vessel attached to vessel with equip!, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.equipment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.overfilled","page":"API reference","text":"overfilled(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver)\n\nThe nodes whose contents exceed the capacity of their Vessel, as a vector of NamedTuples (node, vessel, volume, capacity). The volume of a mash includes the grain it displaces (see mash_volume); volumes are at 20 °C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.overfilled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.brew","page":"API reference","text":"brew(q::Quiver; models = (;))\n\nComputes all the nodes and arrows of q, in topological order, with the models of BrewingFormulas in models (a NamedTuple overriding BrewingFormulas' DEFAULT_MODELS, e.g. (bitterness = Rager(), color = Daniels())). Returns a BrewResult.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.brew"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.rebrew!","page":"API reference","text":"rebrew!(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver, label)\n\nUpdates res after a change of the node label of q (its initial contents) or of the arrows leaving it, recomputing only label and the nodes downstream of it.\n\nOther changes of the structure of q since res was computed are detected and recomputed too: the nodes added, the sources and destinations of the arrows removed (by disconnect! or remove!), and the sources of new arrows. Changes of existing arrows are not detected, so give the label of their source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.rebrew!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.BrewResult","page":"API reference","text":"BrewResult\n\nThe result of brew: res[label] is the Contents of a node, res[source => destination] the ArrowResults of the arrows between two nodes, in their order. See also leftover, leftovers, released and products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.BrewResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.NodeResult","page":"API reference","text":"NodeResult\n\nThe result of a node in a brew: its contents (the initial contents and all that arrived) and the leftover, what no arrow took (spent grain, trub, yeast cake, the products).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.NodeResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.ArrowResult","page":"API reference","text":"ArrowResult\n\nThe result of an arrow in a brew: the contents it moved to its destination and what it released from the quiver, a NamedTuple (water, co2) of masses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.ArrowResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.leftover","page":"API reference","text":"leftover(res::BrewResult, label)\n\nWhat was left in the node label, taken by no arrow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.leftover"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.leftovers","page":"API reference","text":"leftovers(res::BrewResult)\n\nThe nonempty leftovers of all the nodes, as a Dict from label to contents: with what the arrows released (see released), they account for all the initial contents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.leftovers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.released","page":"API reference","text":"released(res::BrewResult)\n\nThe total released from the quiver by its arrows, as a NamedTuple (water, co2) of masses: the water evaporated and the CO₂ escaped (net of the CO₂ taken in by forced carbonation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.released"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.products","page":"API reference","text":"products(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver)\n\nThe contents of the nodes with no arrows leaving them, as a Dict from label to contents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.products"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.duration","page":"API reference","text":"duration(arrow::AbstractArrow)\n\nHow long the arrow takes: the duration of a transfer or a drain, the time of a transformation. A new arrow type with a duration implements this method; the default is no time. See timeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.duration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.timeline","page":"API reference","text":"timeline(q::Quiver; start = 0u\"minute\", models = (;)) -> Timeline\ntimeline(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver; start = 0u\"minute\") -> Timeline\n\nThe timeline of q: when the contents of each node arrive, are all in, and leave, and when each arrow runs, from the durations of the arrows, as early as the arrows allow (the longest paths through the quiver).\n\nThe lots (nodes without predecessors) are ready at start.\nA node is ready when all the streams reaching it (the arrows from other nodes) have arrived. The lots transferred to it without a timing arrive then, e.g. the yeast pitched when the wort is in the fermentor; with a timing (Remaining, At), they arrive during the transformation that follows, e.g. a hop with 10 minutes of boil left.\nA transformation starts when its node is ready; the transfers of amounts and the drain too, and the transfers of the rest when those end, e.g. the grain bed is sparged after the first runnings are drained.\n\nA drain without a duration from a vessel with a drain rate (see Vessel) takes the time to drain the volume it moves, from the brew res of q (brewed with models when not given). Vessels are not checked for being free: see overlaps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.timeline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Timeline","page":"API reference","text":"Timeline\n\nThe result of timeline: s[label] gives the times of a node, a NamedTuple (arrive, ready, leave) (when its contents start arriving, when they are all in, and when they are all gone, nothing for the nodes nothing leaves), and s[source => destination] the Intervals of the arrows between two nodes, in their order. See also makespan, overlaps and vesselchart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Timeline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Interval","page":"API reference","text":"Interval(start, stop)\n\nA time interval, in minutes from the start of the timeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Interval"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.makespan","page":"API reference","text":"makespan(s::Timeline)\n\nThe time from the start of the timeline to the end of its last arrow, or to the arrival of its last contents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.makespan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.overlaps","page":"API reference","text":"overlaps(s::Timeline)\n\nThe pairs of nodes in the same vessel whose times overlap, as a vector of (vessel, node, node): a vessel booked for two contents at once, e.g. one kettle for two boils that are not connected by arrows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.overlaps"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.vesselchart","page":"API reference","text":"vesselchart([io], s::Timeline; from = start, to = end, width = 60)\n\nPrints the timeline as a chart, a row per vessel, between the times from and to: █ where a transformation runs in the vessel, ▒ where it holds contents otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.vesselchart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.solve!","page":"API reference","text":"solve!(q::Quiver, targets...; vary, models = (;), rtol = 1e-8, maxiter = 50) -> Solution\n\nChanges the parameters vary (an AbstractParameter or a vector of them, as many as the targets) of q so that its brew meets the targets (each a Target, or node => f => goal), by Newton's method with a Jacobian by finite differences, damped to keep amounts positive and fractions between 0 and 1. E.g. the grain bill for an original gravity and the sparge water for a pre-boil volume:\n\nsolve!(q, :fermentor => gravity => 1.052u\"sg\", :kettle => volume => 28u\"L\";\n       vary = [Amount(:pale, :crystal), Amount(:sparge_water)])\n\nq is left with the solution, also when the solver does not converge (see the field converged of the Solution).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.solve!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractParameter","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractParameter\n\nSupertype of what solve! varies: Amount and Setting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Amount","page":"API reference","text":"Amount(labels::Symbol...)\n\nThe amount of the lots labels, scaled together by one factor (e.g. a grain bill, keeping its proportions), with the transfers of masses or volumes leaving them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Amount"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Setting","page":"API reference","text":"Setting(source => destination, field; index = 1)\n\nThe numeric field of the index-th arrow from source to destination, e.g. Setting(:kettle => :wort, :stand) for the length of a hop stand, or Setting(:bright1 => :esb, :amount) for the fraction of a blend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Setting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Target","page":"API reference","text":"Target(node, f, goal)\nnode => f => goal\n\nThe goal of solve! that f(contents) of the node (e.g. gravity, volume, ibu, carbonation, or any function of Contents) equals goal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Target"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Solution","page":"API reference","text":"Solution\n\nThe result of solve!: whether it converged, the iterations, the values of the parameters and of the targets reached, and the brew result of the solved quiver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.attribution","page":"API reference","text":"attribution(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver, product) -> Dict{Symbol, Float64}\n\nThe share of each node of q in the product (a node): the fraction of its contents that goes into making the product, following the liquor along the arrows. What a node leaves behind (spent grain, trub, yeast cake, the beer left in a tank) is allocated to the products its other arrows serve. For a lot (a node without predecessors), the share is the fraction of the lot used for the product: what remains on the shelf is not allocated.\n\nThe shares of a lot in all the products of q add up to the fraction of the lot used, unless some of it only reaches nodes whose contents are all left behind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.attribution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.shares","page":"API reference","text":"shares(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver, product) -> Dict{Symbol, Float64}\n\nThe share of each lot (a node without predecessors: an ingredient, the water) used for the product, for the lots with a positive share. See attribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.shares"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.AbstractCalibration","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractCalibration\n\nHow Quiver adjusts a recipe to its stated efficiency: NoCalibration or BrewhouseEfficiency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.AbstractCalibration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.NoCalibration","page":"API reference","text":"NoCalibration()\n\nThe efficiency of a quiver built from a recipe follows from its processes: the conversion of the mash (the recipe's, or 95%), the grain absorption, the dead spaces and losses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.NoCalibration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.BrewhouseEfficiency","page":"API reference","text":"BrewhouseEfficiency()\n\nThe conversion of the mash is adjusted so that the brewhouse efficiency of the quiver (the extract reaching the fermentor over the potential extract of the fermentables of the mash and the boil) is the recipe's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.BrewhouseEfficiency"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.brewhouse_efficiency","page":"API reference","text":"brewhouse_efficiency(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver; into = :fermentor)\n\nThe extract reaching the node into (dissolved, plus the extract equivalent of its alcohol) over the potential extract of the fermentables of the lots of q that reach it through the mash or the boil, i.e. all the fermentables of the lots except those transferred straight into into or after it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.brewhouse_efficiency"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.recipe","page":"API reference","text":"recipe(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver, product; name = string(product), author = \"\",\n       notes = nothing) -> BeerJSON.RecipeType\n\nThe BeerJSON recipe of a product (a node) of the brew res of q. BeerJSON recipes are linear, so the recipe holds the shares of the lots used for the product (see attribution): its ingredient additions, by their stage and timing, and the water of its mash steps and sparges, gathered over the mash tuns that serve it, with the procedures of the mash tun, kettle and fermentor that serve it most. Its batch size is the volume reaching the fermentors for the product, its pre-boil size the volume in the kettles, its efficiency the brewhouse efficiency of those fermentors, and its gravities, alcohol, color and carbonation those of the product, before any priming.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.recipe"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.recipes","page":"API reference","text":"recipes(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver; products = keys of products(res, q), author = \"\")\n\nThe BeerJSON recipes of the products of the brew res of q (see recipe), in the order of the quiver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.recipes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BeerJSON.BeerJSONTypes.writejson","page":"API reference","text":"writejson(path, res::BrewResult, q::Quiver; products = ..., author = \"\")\n\nWrites the BeerJSON recipes of the products of the brew res of q (see recipes) to a BeerJSON document at path.\n\n\n\n\n\nwritejson(path, lib::Library)\n\nWrites the ingredients of lib to a BeerJSON document at path, as fermentables, hop varieties and cultures, with their stock as inventory (counts of cultures are not written: BeerJSON's inventory of cultures holds volumes and masses).\n\n\n\n\n\nwritejson(path_or_io, q::Quiver; results = nothing)\n\nWrites the quiver q as a JSON Graph Format (JGF) document: its nodes (with their initial contents, vessel, and the free data of nodeview), its arrows as edges, in order, its equipment, and its ingredients as BeerJSON catalogue records. With the brew results of q, each node also gets the values computed by it (volume, gravities, alcohol, bitterness, color, carbonation, temperature, and the mash pH of mashes), for other readers of the file; readjson ignores them, and they are computed again by brew.\n\nwritejson(\"bitter.quiver.json\", q; results = brew(q))\nq = readjson(Quiver, \"bitter.quiver.json\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BeerJSON.BeerJSONTypes.writejson"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BeerJSON.BeerJSONTypes.readjson","page":"API reference","text":"readjson(Quiver, path_or_io)\n\nThe quiver of a file written by writejson (see there for the format). The values computed by a brew, if any, are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\nreadjson(Library, path)\n\nThe Library of a BeerJSON document, as Library(path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BeerJSON.BeerJSONTypes.readjson"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.register_json_type","page":"API reference","text":"BrewQuiver.register_json_type(T)\n\nLets the files of quivers (see writejson) write and read the values of the type T, e.g. a new AbstractArrow: as an object of its fields, with its \"type\", rebuilt with its constructor of all its fields in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.register_json_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.write_beerjson","page":"API reference","text":"write_beerjson(args...; kwargs...)\n\nDeprecated: use writejson.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.write_beerjson"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.Library","page":"API reference","text":"Library()\nLibrary(sources...)\n\nA library of ingredients (Fermentable, Hop, Culture) by name, with their stock: a mass, a volume or a count, or nothing when not known. The sources are BeerJSON documents (BeerJSONParser) or the paths of BeerJSON files, whose fermentable, hop and culture records (with their inventory) are loaded in order.\n\nlib[name] is an ingredient, to be used in lots (add!(q, :pale, lib[\"Pale ale malt\"] => 4u\"kg\")); see also stock, lookup, shortages and consume!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.Library"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.stock","page":"API reference","text":"stock(lib::Library, name)\nstock!(lib::Library, name, amount)\n\nThe amount of the ingredient name in stock (nothing when not known), and setting it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.stock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.lookup","page":"API reference","text":"lookup(lib::Library, text)\n\nThe names of the ingredients of lib containing text, ignoring case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.lookup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.requirements","page":"API reference","text":"requirements(res::BrewResult, q::Quiver)\n\nWhat the brew res of q uses of each ingredient, by name: the amounts of the lots taken by their arrows (what is left on the shelf is not used), as masses or counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.requirements"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.shortages","page":"API reference","text":"shortages(lib::Library, res::BrewResult, q::Quiver)\n\nThe ingredients that the brew res of q needs (see requirements) beyond the stock of lib, as a vector of NamedTuples (name, needed, available): those not in the library or without a known stock (available = nothing), and those with less in stock. Amounts that cannot be compared (a count of yeast packs and a volume in stock) are not reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.shortages"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BrewQuiver.consume!","page":"API reference","text":"consume!(lib::Library, res::BrewResult, q::Quiver)\n\nTakes from the stock of lib what the brew res of q uses (see requirements), down to zero, with a warning for what was short. The ingredients not in the library, or without a known or comparable stock, are left as they are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewQuiver.consume!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Mashes,-decoctions,-stands-and-dry-hops","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"All the techniques below are built from the same few arrows; no special process is needed.","title":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Step-mashes","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"A step mash is a chain of MashRests in the mash tun, with infusions of water before them. The first rest converts the grain; the next ones bring the mash to their temperature (the temperature of a node is the temperature of the mixture that reached it).\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :pilsner, pale => 5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :dough_in, water(12u\"L\"; temperature = 57u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :infusion, water(6u\"L\"; temperature = 98u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :mash, :protein_rest, :sacch, :mashout; vessel = :mashtun)\nconnect!(q, [:dough_in, :pilsner] => :mash)\nconnect!(q, :mash => :protein_rest, MashRest(temperature = 52u\"°C\", time = 15u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, [:protein_rest, :infusion] => :sacch)\nconnect!(q, :sacch => :mashout, MashRest(temperature = 66u\"°C\", time = 60u\"minute\"))\nres = brew(q)\n(res[:sacch].liquor.temperature, res[:mashout].liquor.temperature, gravity(res[:mashout]))\n(64.60238904867947 °C, 66.0 °C, 1.0693045780395036 sg)\n\nThe infusion brings the mash to the temperature of the mixture; the rest then holds it at its own temperature, heating as needed.","title":"Step mashes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Decoctions","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"A decoction takes a part of the mash (a Transfer of a fraction) to the kettle, boils it, and returns it before the next rest, which it brings to temperature by mixing:\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :pilsner, pale => 5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :strike, water(15u\"L\"; temperature = 55u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :mash, :rested, :remaining, :sacch, :done; vessel = :mashtun)\nadd!(q, :decoction, :boiled; vessel = :kettle)\nconnect!(q, [:strike, :pilsner] => :mash)\nconnect!(q, :mash => :rested, MashRest(temperature = 52u\"°C\", time = 20u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :rested => :decoction, Transfer(35u\"percent\"))   # the thick part\nconnect!(q, :rested => :remaining)\nconnect!(q, :decoction => :boiled, Boil(time = 15u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, [:remaining, :boiled] => :sacch)\nconnect!(q, :sacch => :done, MashRest(temperature = 66u\"°C\", time = 45u\"minute\"))\nbrew(q)[:sacch].liquor.temperature\n67.05704257819455 °C\n\nThe boil of a decoction evaporates little (10%/hr, BrewQuiver.DECOCTION_EVAPORATION, when the vessel gives no rate).","title":"Decoctions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Hop-stands","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"A hop stand (whirlpool) follows the boil, as part of the Boil:\n\nBoil(time = 60u\"minute\", stand = 20u\"minute\", stand_temperature = 80u\"°C\")\n\nThe hops keep isomerizing during the stand at the rate of its temperature relative to boiling (isomerization_factor of BrewingFormulas). A hop added At(70u\"minute\") stands for the last 10 minutes; one added with remaining = 0u\"minute\" (at flameout) for all of the stand.\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :wort_in, wort(25u\"L\", 1.050u\"sg\"; temperature = 100u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :saaz, saaz => 60u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :kettle, :hopped; vessel = :kettle)\nconnect!(q, :wort_in => :kettle)\nconnect!(q, :saaz => :kettle, Transfer(30u\"g\"; remaining = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :saaz => :kettle, Transfer(30u\"g\"; remaining = 0u\"minute\"))      # flameout\nconnect!(q, :kettle => :hopped, Boil(time = 60u\"minute\", stand = 20u\"minute\", stand_temperature = 80u\"°C\"))\nibu(brew(q)[:hopped])\n13.965042297562553 IBU","title":"Hop stands"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#First-wort-hops-and-additions-by-time","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"Timings are relative to the transformation that follows the node the ingredient arrives at: Remaining(t) with t left of it, At(t) after it starts. An ingredient arriving without timing is in for the whole transformation (first wort hops, the grain in the mash).","title":"First wort hops and additions by time"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Dry-hops","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"Dry hops are additions to the fermentor with a timing (Remaining(3u\"d\") or At(2u\"d\")); they add no bitterness, and keep the beer they absorb when racked, with hop_absorption on the drain or the vessel:\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :wort_in, wort(20u\"L\", 1.060u\"sg\"; temperature = 19u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :citra, citra => 100u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :yeast, Culture(\"US-05\"; attenuation = 78u\"percent\") => 1)\nadd!(q, :fermentor, :beer; vessel = :fermentor)\nadd!(q, :keg)\nconnect!(q, [:wort_in, :yeast] => :fermentor)\nconnect!(q, :citra => :fermentor, Transfer(; remaining = 4u\"d\"))\nconnect!(q, :fermentor => :beer, Ferment(temperature = 19u\"°C\", time = 14u\"d\"))\nconnect!(q, :beer => :keg, Drain(hop_absorption = 5u\"L/kg\"))\nres = brew(q)\n(ibu(res[:keg]), volume(res[:keg]), volume(leftover(res, :beer)))\n(0.0 IBU, 19.387733032760575 L, 0.5000000000000004 L)","title":"Dry hops"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Fly-sparging-and-double-mashes","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"Fly sparging is a sparge with drains in between, or a single drain of the grain bed that receives the sparge water: the quiver does not model the gradient, only the balance of what goes in and out.\nA double mash drains the wort of a mash into another mash, as its strike liquor.","title":"Fly sparging and double mashes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/techniques/#Carbonation","page":"Mashes, decoctions, stands and dry hops","text":"Bottle conditioning is a Prime (the priming sugar in the node is fermented, with its CO₂ dissolved); forced carbonation a Carbonate to a level in volumes. priming_mass gives the sugar for a carbonation.","title":"Carbonation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/files/#Files-of-quivers","page":"Files of quivers","text":"A quiver is saved with writejson and loaded with readjson (the functions of BeerJSON.jl, extended), as a JSON Graph Format (JGF) document, with its ingredients as BeerJSON catalogue records:\n\nwritejson(\"bitter.quiver.json\", q; results = brew(q))\nq = readjson(Quiver, \"bitter.quiver.json\")\n\nBoth also take an IO instead of a path. The file keeps the whole quiver, the order of construction of its nodes, its equipment and the nodeviews, so that a quiver read back brews exactly as the original.","title":"Files of quivers"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/files/#Values-for-other-readers","page":"Files of quivers","text":"With results = brew(q), each node also gets the values computed by the brew (volume, gravity, original gravity, ABV, IBU, color, carbonation, temperature, and the mash pH of mashes), and the graph the names of the models used. Other readers of JGF can show them without a brewing engine. readjson ignores them: they are computed again by brew.","title":"Values for other readers"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/files/#Node-views","page":"Files of quivers","text":"nodeview(q, label) is a Dict{String, Any} of free data for interfaces (a position, a color, a collapsed state, ...), which BrewQuiver does not use but keeps in the files, and keeps when the contents of the node are replaced:\n\nq = single_batch()\nnodeview(q, :mash)[\"position\"] = [0, 1]\npath = writejson(tempname() * \".json\", q)\nnodeview(readjson(Quiver, path), :mash)\nDict{String, Any} with 1 entry:\n  \"position\" => Any[0, 1]","title":"Node views"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/files/#The-format","page":"Files of quivers","text":"A small quiver, a kettle of wort with its hops boiled:\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :wort_in, wort(20u\"L\", 1.045u\"sg\"; temperature = 100u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :goldings, goldings => 30u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :kettle, :hopped; vessel = :kettle)\nconnect!(q, :wort_in => :kettle)\nconnect!(q, :goldings => :kettle, Transfer(; remaining = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :kettle => :hopped, Boil(time = 60u\"minute\", evaporation = 3u\"L/hr\"))\nnodeview(q, :kettle)[\"position\"] = [1, 0]\nio = IOBuffer()\nwritejson(io, q; results = brew(q))\nprint(String(take!(io)))\n{\n  \"graph\": {\n    \"directed\": true,\n    \"edges\": [\n      {\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"arrow\": {\n            \"duration\": {\n              \"unit\": \"min\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"type\": \"Transfer\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"relation\": \"Transfer\",\n        \"source\": \"wort_in\",\n        \"target\": \"kettle\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"arrow\": {\n            \"duration\": {\n              \"unit\": \"min\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"timing\": {\n              \"time\": {\n                \"unit\": \"min\",\n                \"value\": 60.0\n              },\n              \"type\": \"Remaining\"\n            },\n            \"type\": \"Transfer\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"relation\": \"Transfer\",\n        \"source\": \"goldings\",\n        \"target\": \"kettle\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"arrow\": {\n            \"evaporation\": {\n              \"unit\": \"l/hr\",\n              \"value\": 3.0\n            },\n            \"stand\": {\n              \"unit\": \"min\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"stand_temperature\": {\n              \"unit\": \"C\",\n              \"value\": 80.0\n            },\n            \"time\": {\n              \"unit\": \"min\",\n              \"value\": 60.0\n            },\n            \"type\": \"Boil\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"relation\": \"Boil\",\n        \"source\": \"kettle\",\n        \"target\": \"hopped\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"metadata\": {\n      \"brewquiver_version\": \"0.0.13\",\n      \"computed\": {\n        \"brewquiver_version\": \"0.0.13\",\n        \"models\": {\n          \"abv\": \"Balling\",\n          \"bitterness\": \"Tinseth\",\n          \"color\": \"Morey\",\n          \"gravity\": \"SugarGravityQuad\",\n          \"refractometer\": \"TerrillCubic\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"equipment\": {},\n      \"format\": \"BrewQuiver\",\n      \"format_version\": 1,\n      \"ingredients\": {\n        \"cultures\": [],\n        \"fermentables\": [],\n        \"hop_varieties\": [\n          {\n            \"name\": \"East Kent Goldings\",\n            \"form\": \"pellet\",\n            \"alpha_acid\": {\n              \"unit\": \"%\",\n              \"value\": 5\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"nodes\": {\n      \"goldings\": {\n        \"label\": \"goldings\",\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"initial\": {\n            \"liquor\": {\n              \"alcohol\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"co2\": {\n                \"unit\": \"g\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"color\": 0.0,\n              \"extract\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"isoalpha\": {\n                \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"minerals\": {\n                \"alkalinity\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"calcium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"chloride\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"magnesium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"other\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"sodium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"sulfate\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"type\": \"Minerals\"\n              },\n              \"temperature\": {\n                \"unit\": \"C\",\n                \"value\": 20.0\n              },\n              \"type\": \"Liquor\",\n              \"water\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              }\n            },\n            \"solids\": [\n              {\n                \"amount\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.03\n                },\n                \"ingredient\": \"East Kent Goldings\",\n                \"timing\": null,\n                \"type\": \"Portion\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"type\": \"Contents\"\n          },\n          \"rank\": 2\n        }\n      },\n      \"hopped\": {\n        \"label\": \"hopped\",\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"computed\": {\n            \"abv\": {\n              \"unit\": \"%\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"carbonation\": {\n              \"unit\": \"vols\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"color\": {\n              \"unit\": \"SRM\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.0528861607531577\n            },\n            \"ibu\": {\n              \"unit\": \"IBU\",\n              \"value\": 22.600473981769877\n            },\n            \"original_gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.0528861607531577\n            },\n            \"temperature\": {\n              \"unit\": \"C\",\n              \"value\": 100.0\n            },\n            \"volume\": {\n              \"unit\": \"l\",\n              \"value\": 17.000888289001395\n            }\n          },\n          \"rank\": 4,\n          \"vessel\": \"kettle\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"kettle\": {\n        \"label\": \"kettle\",\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"computed\": {\n            \"abv\": {\n              \"unit\": \"%\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"carbonation\": {\n              \"unit\": \"vols\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"color\": {\n              \"unit\": \"SRM\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.045\n            },\n            \"ibu\": {\n              \"unit\": \"IBU\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"original_gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.045\n            },\n            \"temperature\": {\n              \"unit\": \"C\",\n              \"value\": 99.99999999999999\n            },\n            \"volume\": {\n              \"unit\": \"l\",\n              \"value\": 20.0\n            }\n          },\n          \"rank\": 3,\n          \"vessel\": \"kettle\",\n          \"view\": {\n            \"position\": [\n              1,\n              0\n            ]\n          }\n        }\n      },\n      \"wort_in\": {\n        \"label\": \"wort_in\",\n        \"metadata\": {\n          \"computed\": {\n            \"abv\": {\n              \"unit\": \"%\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"carbonation\": {\n              \"unit\": \"vols\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"color\": {\n              \"unit\": \"SRM\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.045\n            },\n            \"ibu\": {\n              \"unit\": \"IBU\",\n              \"value\": 0.0\n            },\n            \"original_gravity\": {\n              \"unit\": \"sg\",\n              \"value\": 1.045\n            },\n            \"temperature\": {\n              \"unit\": \"C\",\n              \"value\": 100.0\n            },\n            \"volume\": {\n              \"unit\": \"l\",\n              \"value\": 20.0\n            }\n          },\n          \"initial\": {\n            \"liquor\": {\n              \"alcohol\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"co2\": {\n                \"unit\": \"g\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"color\": 0.0,\n              \"extract\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 2.3355642816332884\n              },\n              \"isoalpha\": {\n                \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                \"value\": 0.0\n              },\n              \"minerals\": {\n                \"alkalinity\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"calcium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"chloride\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"magnesium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"other\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"sodium\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"sulfate\": {\n                  \"unit\": \"mg\",\n                  \"value\": 0.0\n                },\n                \"type\": \"Minerals\"\n              },\n              \"temperature\": {\n                \"unit\": \"C\",\n                \"value\": 100.0\n              },\n              \"type\": \"Liquor\",\n              \"water\": {\n                \"unit\": \"kg\",\n                \"value\": 18.52687841836671\n              }\n            },\n            \"solids\": [],\n            \"type\": \"Contents\"\n          },\n          \"rank\": 1\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"type\": \"brewquiver\"\n  }\n}\n\nThe document is a JGF graph:\n\n\"graph\": \"type\": \"brewquiver\", \"directed\": true, and\n\"metadata\":\n\"format\": \"BrewQuiver\", \"format_version\" (1; a newer version is refused by older readers), \"brewquiver_version\";\n\"ingredients\": \"fermentables\", \"hop_varieties\" and \"cultures\", as BeerJSON catalogue records (FermentableType, VarietyInformation, CultureInformation), by name. The buffering of a fermentable is an extra property, \"buffering\". Two different ingredients cannot have the same name in a file.\n\"equipment\": the vessels by name;\n\"computed\" (optional): the \"models\" of the brew.\n\"nodes\": by label, each with a \"label\" and a \"metadata\" holding\n\"rank\": the order of construction;\n\"vessel\", if any;\n\"initial\": the initial contents, omitted for the stages that start empty;\n\"view\": the node view, if not empty;\n\"computed\" (optional): the values of the brew.\n\"edges\": one per arrow, in order, with \"source\", \"target\", \"relation\" (the type of the arrow) and \"metadata\": {\"arrow\": ...}.\n\nQuantities are written as in BeerJSON, {\"unit\": ..., \"value\": ...}, in the units kg, g, mg, l, ml, min, hr, day, C, l/kg, l/hr, 1/hr, l/min, vols, SRM, mg/l, g/ml, sg, IBU and %. The other values (arrows, timings, liquors, minerals, solids, vessels, priming sugars) are objects of their fields with their \"type\"; a Portion refers to its ingredient by name. New arrow types are written once registered; see Extending BrewQuiver.\n\nA JSON Schema of the format is planned.","title":"The format"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Extending-BrewQuiver","page":"Extending BrewQuiver","text":"","title":"Extending BrewQuiver"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-new-transformation","page":"Extending BrewQuiver","text":"A transformation is a subtype of AbstractTransformation that implements transform(t, contents, models) -> (contents, released): the contents after it, and what it released from the quiver, as a NamedTuple (water, co2) of masses (negative for what it took in). With a time field, its duration is that time; otherwise it implements duration.\n\nA cold crash, which only cools the beer:\n\nstruct ColdCrash <: BrewQuiver.AbstractTransformation\n    temperature::typeof(1.0u\"°C\")\n    time::typeof(1.0u\"d\")\nend\nColdCrash(; temperature = 2u\"°C\", time = 2u\"d\") = ColdCrash(temperature, time)\n\nBrewQuiver.transform(t::ColdCrash, c::Contents, models) =\n    (with_temperature(c, t.temperature), (water = 0.0u\"kg\", co2 = 0.0u\"kg\"))\n\nq = single_batch()\nadd!(q, :crashed; vessel = :fermentor)\ndisconnect!(q, :beer => :bottling)\nconnect!(q, :beer => :crashed, ColdCrash())\nconnect!(q, :crashed => :bottling, Drain())\nliquor(brew(q)[:crashed]).temperature\n2.0 °C\n\nThe transformation should conserve mass: what it changes in the contents is either released (water, co2) or moved within them (extract to alcohol, ...). The tests of BrewQuiver check the balance of the whole quiver with leftovers and released; do the same for a new arrow.\n\nThe formulas a transformation needs belong in BrewingFormulas.jl, with their references.","title":"A new transformation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Saving-it","page":"Extending BrewQuiver","text":"The files of quivers write the arrows they know. A new type is registered with BrewQuiver.register_json_type: its values are then written as objects of their fields, and rebuilt with its constructor of all the fields, in order.\n\nBrewQuiver.register_json_type(ColdCrash)\npath = writejson(tempname() * \".json\", q)\nreadjson(Quiver, path)[:crashed => :bottling]\n1-element Vector{AbstractArrow}:\n Drain()","title":"Saving it"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Models","page":"Extending BrewQuiver","text":"The models of a brew are those of BrewingFormulas (brew(q; models = (bitterness = Rager(),))). A new model is a new subtype there, with its methods; the quiver uses it as soon as it is passed to brew.","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Timings-and-solids","page":"Extending BrewQuiver","text":"New timings (AbstractTiming) and solids (AbstractSolid) are possible, but touch the internals of the arrows (how they mix, split and dissolve); open an issue first.","title":"Timings and solids"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/water/#Water-chemistry","page":"Water chemistry","text":"Liquors carry their dissolved Minerals: calcium, magnesium, sodium, chloride, sulfate and alkalinity (as CaCO₃, negative once acids took more than it), conserved like everything else. They mix, drain with the liquor, and concentrate in the boil.","title":"Water chemistry"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/water/#Water-profiles","page":"Water chemistry","text":"A water is given by its WaterProfile, the concentrations of its ions, as in a water report:\n\ntap = WaterProfile(calcium = 30u\"mg/L\", magnesium = 8u\"mg/L\", sodium = 12u\"mg/L\",\n                   chloride = 25u\"mg/L\", sulfate = 30u\"mg/L\", bicarbonate = 120u\"mg/L\")\nw = water(16u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\", profile = tap)\nw.minerals\nMinerals(480.0 mg, 128.0 mg, 192.0 mg, 400.0 mg, 480.0 mg, 1574.7108955028189 mg, 1920.0 mg)\n\nprofile gives the profile of any liquor or contents, and residual_alkalinity Kolbach's residual alkalinity:\n\n(profile(w), residual_alkalinity(w))\n(WaterProfile(Ca 30.0, Mg 8.0, Na 12.0, Cl 25.0, SO₄ 30.0, HCO₃ 120.0 mg/L), 72.30797908389134 mg L^-1)","title":"Water profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/water/#Salts-and-acids","page":"Water chemistry","text":"Salts and Acids are lots that dissolve where they are added: their ions go to the liquor, and an acid takes alkalinity. The salts and acids are those of BrewingFormulas (Gypsum, CalciumChloride, EpsomSalt, MagnesiumChloride, TableSalt, BakingSoda, Chalk, SlakedLime, LacticAcid, PhosphoricAcid).\n\nq = single_batch()\nq[:strike_water] = water(16u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\", profile = tap)\nadd!(q, :gypsum, Salt(Gypsum()) => 4u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :lactic, Acid(LacticAcid()) => 1u\"mL\")\nconnect!(q, [:gypsum, :lactic] => :mash)\nres = brew(q)\nprofile(res[:wort])\nWaterProfile(Ca 56.4, Mg 5.1, Na 7.7, Cl 16.0, SO₄ 108.3, HCO₃ 48.7 mg/L)","title":"Salts and acids"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/water/#Mash-pH","page":"Water chemistry","text":"mash_ph estimates the pH of the mash of a node before its mash rest, by the balance of charges of BrewingFormulas' mash_ph: the distilled water pH and buffering of each grain, and the residual alkalinity and volume of the water.\n\nmash_ph(res[:mash])\n5.62892077172718\n\nPale base malt is taken with a distilled water pH of 5.7 and a buffering of 40 mEq/(kg·pH). Crystal and roasted malts lower the pH much more, so they need their own distilled water pH (Fermentable(...; di_ph = 4.7), with an optional buffering); a grain darker than 8 SRM without one is taken as base malt, with a warning.","title":"Mash pH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/water/#From-BeerJSON","page":"Water chemistry","text":"The importer reads the water additions of a recipe as the profile of its water, its \"water agent\" additions as salts and acids, and the distilled water pH of fermentable records.","title":"From BeerJSON"},{"category":"section","location":"#BrewQuiver.jl","page":"Home","text":"The brewing process as a quiver.\n\nBrewQuiver is the engine of the BrewQuiver brewing software, in pure Julia. It describes the brewing process as a quiver: a directed acyclic multigraph whose nodes are the contents of vessels at the stages of the brewing (the lots of malt and hops, the water, the mash, the wort in the kettle, the beer in the fermentor, ...) and whose arrows are the processes between them: transfers, drains, mash rests, boils, fermentations, conditioning.\n\nUnlike a linear recipe, a quiver can split and merge streams, as in parti-gyle brewing, double mashes or blending. And since the state of every node holds conserved quantities only, mixing and splitting are exact, and every gram of water, extract and hop is accounted for over the whole brew.\n\n<pre style=\"line-height:1.25\">\n  pale ─┐                    goldings ─┐\ncrystal ─┼─▶ mash ─MashRest─▶ rested ─Drain─▶ kettle ─Boil─▶ wort ─Chill─▶ chilled ─Drain─▶ fermentor ─Ferment─▶ beer\n water ─┘                       │               ▲                                         ▲\n                                └─▶ grain_bed ─Drain┘                                    yeast\n                    sparge water ──▶┘\n</pre>","title":"BrewQuiver.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Exact mass balance. Water, extract, alcohol, iso-alpha acids, CO₂, color units and dissolved minerals are conserved: what no arrow takes is kept as a leftover (spent grain, trub, yeast cake), and what the arrows release (evaporated water, CO₂) is counted.\nDerived quantities with BrewingFormulas.jl: volume, gravity, original gravity, ABV, IBU, color, carbonation, mash pH, with models chosen per brew (Tinseth or Rager, Morey, ...).\nUnits everywhere with Unitful.jl and UnitfulBrewing.jl: 4.5u\"kg\", 1.052u\"sg\", 12u\"°P\", 35u\"IBU\", 2.4u\"vols\".\nBrewing techniques: batch and fly sparging, step mashes, decoctions, hop stands, dry hops, bottle priming and forced carbonation, parti-gyles and blends.\nEquipment: capacities, dead spaces, grain absorption, evaporation and drain rates.\nWater chemistry: mineral profiles, salts and acids, residual alkalinity, mash pH.\nTimeline of the brew day and the fermentation, per vessel.\nTargets: solve the amounts and settings that give a gravity, a bitterness, a volume.\nProvenance: the share of every lot in every product.\nBeerJSON: import recipes as quivers, export quivers as recipes, ingredient libraries with inventory, with BeerJSON.jl.\nFiles of quivers in the JSON Graph Format, with the ingredients as BeerJSON records.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"BrewQuiver is under development and not yet registered. It needs Julia 1.11 or later, and takes its sibling packages from GitHub through the [sources] of its Project.toml:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/BrewQuiver.jl\")\n\nFor the examples you will also want the units and the formulas:\n\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/UnitfulBrewing.jl\")\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/BrewingFormulas.jl\")\nPkg.add(\"Unitful\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-quiver","page":"Home","text":"using Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BrewQuiver\n\npale    = Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"; yield = 80u\"percent\", color = 3u\"°L\")\ncascade = Hop(\"Cascade\"; alpha = 6u\"percent\")\nus05    = Culture(\"US-05\"; attenuation = 78u\"percent\")\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :pale, pale => 4.5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :cascade, cascade => 30u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :yeast, us05 => 1)\nadd!(q, :water, water(26u\"L\"; temperature = 72u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :mash, :wort; vessel = :mashtun)\nadd!(q, :kettle, :hopped; vessel = :kettle)\nadd!(q, :fermentor, :beer; vessel = :fermentor)\n\nconnect!(q, [:water, :pale] => :mash)\nconnect!(q, :mash => :wort, MashRest(temperature = 66u\"°C\"))\nconnect!(q, :wort => :kettle, Drain(absorption = 1u\"L/kg\"))\nconnect!(q, :cascade => :kettle, Transfer(; remaining = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :kettle => :hopped, Boil(time = 60u\"minute\", evaporation = 4u\"L/hr\"))\nconnect!(q, :hopped => :fermentor, Drain(retain = 1u\"L\"))\nconnect!(q, :yeast => :fermentor)\nconnect!(q, :fermentor => :beer, Ferment(temperature = 19u\"°C\", time = 14u\"d\"))\n\nres = brew(q)\nres[:beer]\nLiquor: 18.61 L at 19.0 °C\n  gravity:     1.0115 sg (2.93 °P apparent, 4.81 °P real)\n  original:    1.0539 sg\n  alcohol:     5.65% ABV, 4.42% ABW\n  bitterness:  23.4 IBU\n  color:       4.4 SRM\n  carbonation: 0.89 vols\n  masses:      17.059 kg water, 0.903 kg extract, 0.831 kg alcohol\nSolids: 91.4 g yeast\n\n(original_gravity(res[:beer]), gravity(res[:beer]), abv(res[:beer]), ibu(res[:beer]), srm(res[:beer]))\n(1.053900480875303 sg, 1.011453830544521 sg, 5.653504818660545 %, 23.422311058283125 IBU, 4.394233081689053 SRM)\n\nContinue with the Concepts, then the tutorials.","title":"A first quiver"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-packages","page":"Home","text":"UnitfulBrewing.jl: brewing units.\nBrewingFormulas.jl: the formulas and models, with their references.\nBeerJSON.jl: reading and writing BeerJSON.","title":"Related packages"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#A-single-batch","page":"A single batch","text":"This tutorial builds the quiver of an all-grain English bitter, step by step: a mash with a batch sparge, a boil with two hop additions, a fermentation and bottle conditioning. It then brews it, reads the results, and checks where every gram went.","title":"A single batch"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#The-ingredients","page":"A single batch","text":"Ingredients are working types built from their brewing parameters, with units:\n\nusing Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas, BrewQuiver\n\npale     = Fermentable(\"Pale ale malt\"; yield = 80u\"percent\", color = 3u\"°L\")\ncrystal  = Fermentable(\"Crystal 60\"; yield = 74u\"percent\", color = 60u\"°L\")\ngoldings = Hop(\"East Kent Goldings\"; alpha = 5u\"percent\")\nale      = Culture(\"English ale\"; attenuation = 75u\"percent\")\ndextrose = Fermentable(\"Corn sugar\"; kind = :sugar, yield = 100u\"percent\", color = 0u\"SRM\",\n                       moisture = 0)\n\nA Fermentable has a kind (:grain by default, :sugar, :extract, ...), a yield (fine grind, as is), a color and a moisture (4% for grains by default). A Hop has its alpha acids and form (pellets by default); a Culture its apparent attenuation.","title":"The ingredients"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#The-lots","page":"A single batch","text":"The lots are the sources of the quiver: an ingredient with its amount, or a liquor.\n\nq = Quiver()\nadd!(q, :pale, pale => 4.5u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :crystal, crystal => 0.3u\"kg\")\nadd!(q, :goldings, goldings => 50u\"g\")\nadd!(q, :yeast, ale => 1)                                   # one pack\nadd!(q, :strike_water, water(16u\"L\"; temperature = 75u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :sparge_water, water(14u\"L\"; temperature = 80u\"°C\"))\nadd!(q, :priming_sugar, dextrose => 110u\"g\")","title":"The lots"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#The-stages","page":"A single batch","text":"Each vessel goes through stages, empty nodes filled by the arrows. The mash tun holds the mash (grain and water), the rested mash (converted), then the grain bed being sparged; the kettle holds the wort before and after the boil, then chilled:\n\nadd!(q, :mash, :rested, :grain_bed; vessel = :mashtun)\nadd!(q, :kettle, :wort, :chilled; vessel = :kettle)\nadd!(q, :fermentor, :beer; vessel = :fermentor)\nadd!(q, :bottling, :bottles; vessel = :bottles)","title":"The stages"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#The-equipment","page":"A single batch","text":"The Vessels give what the arrows leaving their stages do not say: the grain absorption and dead space of the mash tun, the evaporation rate of the kettle, the drain rates (see Equipment).\n\nequip!(q, :mashtun, Vessel(name = \"Cooler\", capacity = 36u\"L\", absorption = 1.0u\"L/kg\",\n                           dead_space = 0.5u\"L\", drain_rate = 1u\"L/minute\"))\nequip!(q, :kettle, Vessel(name = \"Kettle\", capacity = 36u\"L\", evaporation = 4u\"L/hr\",\n                          dead_space = 1u\"L\", drain_rate = 1.5u\"L/minute\"))\nequip!(q, :fermentor, Vessel(name = \"Bucket\", capacity = 30u\"L\", dead_space = 1u\"L\"))","title":"The equipment"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#The-arrows","page":"A single batch","text":"The mash: the strike water and the grain go to the mash tun, which rests at 67 °C. The first runnings are drained to the kettle; the grain, with the wort it absorbed, stays as the grain bed, which gets the sparge water and is drained again (a batch sparge).\n\nconnect!(q, [:strike_water, :pale, :crystal] => :mash)\nconnect!(q, :mash => :rested, MashRest(temperature = 67u\"°C\", time = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :rested => :kettle, Drain())                    # first runnings\nconnect!(q, :rested => :grain_bed)                          # the rest: grain and absorbed wort\nconnect!(q, :sparge_water => :grain_bed)\nconnect!(q, :grain_bed => :kettle, Drain())                 # second runnings\n\nThe boil: two additions from the same lot of hops, with 60 and 10 minutes of boil left. They are two arrows between the same nodes, which is why the graph is a multigraph.\n\nconnect!(q, :goldings => :kettle, Transfer(30u\"g\"; remaining = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :goldings => :kettle, Transfer(20u\"g\"; remaining = 10u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :kettle => :wort, Boil(time = 60u\"minute\"))\nconnect!(q, :wort => :chilled, Chill(temperature = 20u\"°C\", time = 25u\"minute\"))\n\nThe fermentation and the bottling: the chilled wort is drained off the trub and hops, the yeast is pitched, and the beer is drained off its yeast cake onto the priming sugar.\n\nconnect!(q, :chilled => :fermentor, Drain())\nconnect!(q, :yeast => :fermentor)\nconnect!(q, :fermentor => :beer, Ferment(temperature = 20u\"°C\", time = 14u\"d\"))\nconnect!(q, :priming_sugar => :bottling)\nconnect!(q, :beer => :bottling, Drain())\nconnect!(q, :bottling => :bottles, Prime(sugar = CornSugar()))\nq\nQuiver with 17 nodes and 18 arrows\n  lots\n    :pale  4.5 kg Pale ale malt\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :crystal  300.0 g Crystal 60\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :goldings  50.0 g East Kent Goldings\n        Transfer(30.0 g, 60.0 min remaining) → :kettle\n        Transfer(20.0 g, 10.0 min remaining) → :kettle\n    :yeast  1 × English ale\n        Transfer(all) → :fermentor\n    :strike_water  16.0 L, 1.0 sg, 75.0 °C\n        Transfer(all) → :mash\n    :sparge_water  14.0 L, 1.0 sg, 80.0 °C\n        Transfer(all) → :grain_bed\n    :priming_sugar  110.0 g Corn sugar\n        Transfer(all) → :bottling\n  [mashtun]  Vessel(\"Cooler\"; 36.0 L, 0.5 L dead space, 1.0 L/kg absorbed, draining 1.0 L/min)\n    :mash\n        MashRest(67.0 °C, 60.0 min) → :rested\n    :rested\n        Transfer(rest) → :grain_bed\n        Drain() → :kettle\n    :grain_bed\n        Drain() → :kettle\n  [kettle]  Vessel(\"Kettle\"; 36.0 L, 1.0 L dead space, evaporating 4.0 L/h, draining 1.5 L/min)\n    :kettle\n        Boil(60.0 min) → :wort\n    :wort\n        Chill(20.0 °C, 25.0 min) → :chilled\n    :chilled\n        Drain() → :fermentor\n  [fermentor]  Vessel(\"Bucket\"; 30.0 L, 1.0 L dead space)\n    :fermentor\n        Ferment(20.0 °C, 14.0 d) → :beer\n    :beer\n        Drain() → :bottling\n  [bottles]\n    :bottling\n        Prime(CornSugar, 14.0 d) → :bottles\n    :bottles","title":"The arrows"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#Brewing","page":"A single batch","text":"brew computes every node, in topological order:\n\nres = brew(q)\nBrewResult\n  lots\n    :pale  4.5 kg Pale ale malt\n    :crystal  300.0 g Crystal 60\n    :goldings  50.0 g East Kent Goldings\n    :yeast  1 × English ale\n    :strike_water  16.0 L, 1.0 sg, 75.0 °C\n    :sparge_water  14.0 L, 1.0 sg, 80.0 °C\n    :priming_sugar  110.0 g Corn sugar\n  [mashtun]\n    :mash  16.0 L, 1.0 sg, 75.0 °C; 4.5 kg Pale ale malt, 300.0 g Crystal 60\n    :rested  18.35 L, 1.073 sg, 9.3 SRM, 67.0 °C; 1.122 kg spent grain (from 4.8 kg grain)\n    :grain_bed  19.3 L, 1.0202 sg, 3.8 SRM, 76.2 °C; 1.122 kg spent grain (from 4.8 kg grain)\n        left: 5.3 L, 1.0202 sg, 3.8 SRM, 76.2 °C; 1.122 kg spent grain (from 4.8 kg grain)\n  [kettle]\n    :kettle  27.04 L, 1.0457 sg, 6.7 SRM, 71.7 °C; 30.0 g East Kent Goldings (60.0 min remaining), 20.0 g East Kent Goldings (10.0 min remaining)\n    :wort  23.04 L, 1.0536 sg, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 100.0 °C; 49.5 g hop matter\n    :chilled  23.04 L, 1.0536 sg, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 20.0 °C; 49.5 g hop matter\n        left: 1.0 L, 1.0536 sg, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 20.0 °C; 49.5 g hop matter\n  [fermentor]\n    :fermentor  22.04 L, 1.0536 sg, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 20.0 °C; 1 × English ale\n    :beer  21.94 L, 1.013 sg (OG 1.0536), 5.41% ABV, 20.7 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 0.86 vols, 20.0 °C; 103.1 g yeast\n        left: 1.0 L, 1.013 sg (OG 1.0536), 5.41% ABV, 20.7 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 0.86 vols, 20.0 °C; 103.1 g yeast\n  [bottles]\n    :bottling  20.94 L, 1.013 sg (OG 1.0536), 5.41% ABV, 20.7 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 0.86 vols, 20.0 °C; 110.0 g Corn sugar\n    :bottles  21.01 L, 1.0125 sg (OG 1.0553), 5.7% ABV, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 2.04 vols, 20.0 °C\n\nEach node is a Contents, and the usual quantities derive from it:\n\nres[:kettle]\nLiquor: 27.04 L at 71.7 °C\n  gravity:     1.0457 sg (11.37 °P apparent, 11.37 °P real)\n  original:    1.0457 sg\n  alcohol:     0.0% ABV, 0.0% ABW\n  bitterness:  0.0 IBU\n  color:       6.7 SRM\n  carbonation: 0.0 vols\n  masses:      25.017 kg water, 3.209 kg extract, 0.0 kg alcohol\nSolids: 30.0 g East Kent Goldings (60.0 min remaining), 20.0 g East Kent Goldings (10.0 min remaining)\n\nbeer = res[:bottles]\n(volume = volume(beer), og = original_gravity(beer), fg = gravity(beer),\n abv = abv(beer), ibu = ibu(beer), color = srm(beer), co2 = carbonation(beer))\n(volume = 21.00704350834883 L, og = 1.055334161217156 sg, fg = 1.0125386647372585 sg, abv = 5.704694168316063 %, ibu = 20.600786952768022 IBU, color = 7.495278870244144 SRM, co2 = 2.0350518043168946 vols)\n\nWhat happened along an arrow is in the ArrowResults, e.g. the first runnings and the water evaporated by the boil:\n\nonly(res[:rested => :kettle]).moved\nLiquor: 13.05 L at 67.0 °C\n  gravity:     1.073 sg (17.74 °P apparent, 17.74 °P real)\n  original:    1.073 sg\n  alcohol:     0.0% ABV, 0.0% ABW\n  bitterness:  0.0 IBU\n  color:       9.3 SRM\n  carbonation: 0.0 vols\n  masses:      11.494 kg water, 2.479 kg extract, 0.0 kg alcohol\n\nonly(res[:kettle => :wort]).released\n(water = 3.992812 kg, co2 = 0.0 kg)","title":"Brewing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#Where-everything-went","page":"A single batch","text":"What no arrow takes from a node is its leftover: the spent grain with the wort it holds, the trub and the hops, the yeast cake, and the products themselves.\n\nleftovers(res)\nDict{Symbol, Contents} with 4 entries:\n  :bottles   => Contents(21.01 L, 1.0125 sg (OG 1.0553), 5.7% ABV, 20.6 IBU, 7.…\n  :grain_bed => Contents(5.3 L, 1.0202 sg, 3.8 SRM, 76.2 °C; 1.122 kg spent gra…\n  :chilled   => Contents(1.0 L, 1.0536 sg, 20.6 IBU, 7.5 SRM, 20.0 °C; 49.5 g h…\n  :beer      => Contents(1.0 L, 1.013 sg (OG 1.0536), 5.41% ABV, 20.7 IBU, 7.5 …\n\nWith what the arrows released, evaporated water and CO₂, the masses add up exactly to the lots:\n\ninitial = sum(total_mass(q[n].initial) for n in nodes(q))\nleft = sum(total_mass, values(leftovers(res)))\nr = released(res)\ninitial ≈ left + r.water + r.co2\ntrue","title":"Where everything went"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#Changing-the-brew","page":"A single batch","text":"Arrows and lots can be replaced, and rebrew! recomputes only what is downstream:\n\nq[:kettle => :wort] = Boil(time = 90u\"minute\")\nrebrew!(res, q, :kettle)\n(volume(res[:bottles]), original_gravity(res[:bottles]), ibu(res[:bottles]))\n(19.006252605813405 L, 1.0605529710499653 sg, 22.0554214246574 IBU)","title":"Changing the brew"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_batch/#Next","page":"A single batch","text":"The timeline of this brew, and its targets.\nIts water chemistry and mash pH.\nSaving it: Files of quivers.","title":"Next"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/timeline/#Timeline","page":"Timeline","text":"Every arrow has a duration: the time of a transformation (the mash rest, the boil, the fermentation, the conditioning), and the duration of a transfer or a drain (given, or from the drain rate of the vessel).\n\ntimeline places them as early as the quiver allows:\n\na node is ready when all the streams reaching it have arrived;\nthe lots without timing arrive then (the yeast is pitched when the wort is in);\nthe timed additions arrive during the transformation that follows (a hop with 10 minutes of boil left);\nthe transfers of the rest follow the drains (the grain bed is sparged after the first runnings).\n\nIt gives when each node is filled, ready and emptied:\n\ns = timeline(q; start = 8u\"hr\")\nTimeline over 28d 3:07\n                      filled   ready       emptied\n  [mashtun]\n    :mash               8:00 → 8:00      → 9:00\n    :rested             9:00 → 9:00      → 9:13\n    :grain_bed          9:13 → 9:13      → 9:27\n  [kettle]\n    :kettle             9:00 → 9:27      → 10:27\n    :wort              10:27 → 10:27     → 10:52\n    :chilled           10:52 → 10:52     → 11:07\n  [fermentor]\n    :fermentor         10:52 → 11:07     → 14d 11:07\n    :beer          14d 11:07 → 14d 11:07 → 14d 11:07\n  [bottles]\n    :bottling      14d 11:07 → 14d 11:07 → 28d 11:07\n    :bottles       28d 11:07 → 28d 11:07 → \n\ns[:kettle].arrive, s[:kettle].ready, s[:kettle].leave      # in minutes from midnight\n(540.0 minute, 567.0425539794726 minute, 627.0425539794726 minute)\n\nvesselchart draws the occupation of each vessel (filling ▒, busy █):\n\nvesselchart(s; to = 13u\"hr\")\n            8:00                                                   13:00\n  mashtun   ████████████▒▒▒▒▒\n  kettle                ▒▒▒▒▒█████████████████▒▒▒\n  fermentor                                   ▒▒▒███████████████████████\n  bottles\n\nmakespan is the total time, and overlaps flags a vessel booked for two contents at once, as when a parti-gyle sends two worts to the same copper:\n\n(uconvert(u\"d\", makespan(s)), overlaps(s))\n(28.129678636420493 d, Tuple{Symbol, Symbol, Symbol}[])\n\nThe timeline does not yet take shared resources into account (one brewer, one burner); it is the earliest schedule the processes allow.","title":"Timeline"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The formulas of BrewQuiver are those of BrewingFormulas.jl, whose REFERENCES.md lists their sources: the conversions between gravity and extract, hop utilization (Tinseth, Rager) and isomerization during hop stands, color (Morey, Daniels, Mosher), alcohol (Balling), priming and carbonation, water chemistry and mash pH (Kolbach, and the charge balance of the mash).\n\nThe mass balance of fermentation and the original extract of fermented liquors follow Balling's relations:\n\nJ. De Clerck, A Textbook of Brewing, Chapman & Hall, 1958.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers: Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995.\nJ. Šavel et al., Kvasny prumysl 66 (2020) 239–244.\n\nData formats:\n\nBeerJSON, the JSON format for brewing recipes.\nJSON Graph Format, for the files of quivers.\n\nThe design of BrewQuiver, brewing as a quiver of contents and processes, is described in the BrewQuiver notes.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/equipment/#Equipment","page":"Equipment","text":"A Vessel describes the equipment of a vessel:\n\nField What it is\ncapacity the volume it holds, checked by overfilled\ndead_space the liquid left behind when drained (under a false bottom, the trub in a kettle, the yeast cake)\nabsorption the liquid absorbed by the grain, per mass of grain (a mash tun)\nhop_absorption the liquid absorbed by hops, per mass of hops (a kettle, a fermentor with dry hops)\nevaporation the evaporation rate of a boil: a volume per hour, or a fraction per hour\ndrain_rate the volume drained per unit of time\n\nequip! attaches it to the stages tagged with that vessel (add!(...; vessel = :kettle)). The arrows leaving them take from it what they do not give themselves:\n\na plain Drain() leaves the grain absorption and the dead space behind, and takes the time of the drain rate;\nBoil(time = 60u\"minute\") evaporates at the rate of the kettle.\n\nAn arrow that gives its own value overrides the vessel: Drain(retain = 2u\"L\"), Boil(evaporation = 5u\"L/hr\").\n\nq = single_batch()\nequipment(q, :kettle)\nVessel(\"Kettle\"; 36.0 L, 1.0 L dead space, evaporating 4.0 L/h, draining 1.5 L/min)\n\nres = brew(q)\nvolume(res[:kettle]) - volume(res[:wort])     # 4 L/hr for an hour, and the cooling shrinkage\n3.9987913288816586 L","title":"Equipment"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/equipment/#Capacity","page":"Equipment","text":"overfilled lists the stages whose contents exceed the capacity of their vessel (with the volume the grain displaces in a mash):\n\nequip!(q, :fermentor, Vessel(name = \"Small bucket\", capacity = 20u\"L\"))\nforeach(println, overfilled(brew(q), q))\n(node = :fermentor, vessel = :fermentor, volume = 22.04202369904633 L, capacity = 20.0 L)\n(node = :beer, vessel = :fermentor, volume = 21.937745291907937 L, capacity = 20.0 L)","title":"Capacity"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/equipment/#From-BeerJSON","page":"Equipment","text":"The equipment items of a BeerJSON document (mash tun, kettle, fermentor, ...) become the vessels of the quiver of a recipe; see A BeerJSON recipe.","title":"From BeerJSON"}]
}
